/* The rubrics of the Missale O.P. (1939) in English, by paragraph (ids as in rubrics.html), as HTML with the prayers in bold; built by tools/rubrics/build_rubrics.py.
   The Mass page loads this when a direction is opened. */
window.RUBTEXT = {"defectus-i-1":["Of defects occurring in the celebration of Mass","The priest who is to celebrate shall take every care that nothing required for confecting the Sacrament of the Eucharist be lacking. A defect may occur on the part of the matter to be consecrated, of the form to be used, and of the minister who confects it. For if any of these is lacking, namely the due matter, the form with the intention, and the priestly Order in him who confects, the Sacrament is not confected. And when these are present, whatever else may be lacking, the reality of the Sacrament is there. There are other defects, however, which occurring in the celebration of Mass, though they do not prevent the reality of the Sacrament, may yet occur with sin or with scandal."],"defectus-ii-1":["Of defects of the matter","Defects on the part of the matter may occur if anything is lacking of what it requires. For it is required that it be wheaten bread and wine of the grape, and that this matter to be consecrated be before the priest in the act of consecration."],"defectus-iii-1":["Of a defect of the bread","1. If the bread is not wheaten, or if wheaten is mixed with grain of another kind in such quantity that it does not remain wheaten bread, or is otherwise corrupt, the Sacrament is not confected. If it is made with rose-water or some other distilled water, it is doubtful whether it is confected."],"defectus-iii-2":["Of a defect of the bread","2. If it has begun to corrupt but is not corrupt, and likewise if it is not unleavened according to the custom of the Latin Church, the Sacrament is confected, but he who confects it sins gravely."],"defectus-iii-3":["Of a defect of the bread","3. If before the consecration the celebrant notices that the host is corrupt, or not wheaten, he shall remove that host, put another, and having made the offering, at least in his mind, continue from the place where he left off. If he notices it after the consecration, even after consuming that host, he shall put another, make the offering as above, and begin from the consecration, that is from the words <b class=\"rb-inc\">Qui pridie</b> quam pateretur; and the former host, if he has not consumed it, he shall consume after the Body and Blood, or give it to others to consume, or keep it reverently somewhere. If he had consumed it, he shall nonetheless consume the one he has consecrated, for the precept of completing the Sacrament weighs more than that of receiving it fasting."],"defectus-iii-4":["Of a defect of the bread","4. It is to be noted that if this happens after the consumption of the Blood, new bread and wine with water must be set out again, and after first making the offering as above, the priest shall consecrate beginning from the words <b class=\"rb-inc\">Qui pridie</b>, and at once consume both and continue the Mass, lest the Sacrament remain incomplete, and that the due order be kept."],"defectus-iii-5":["Of a defect of the bread","5. If the consecrated host disappears, whether by some chance such as the wind, or by a miracle, or is taken by some animal, and cannot be found, another shall be consecrated, beginning from <b class=\"rb-inc\">Qui pridie</b> quam pateretur, after first offering it as above; and the animal, if it can be caught, shall be killed and burnt, and the ashes thrown into the sacrarium or under the altar."],"defectus-iv-1":["Of a defect of the wine","1. If the wine has turned entirely to vinegar, or is entirely putrid, or is pressed from sour or unripe grapes, or so much water has been mixed with it that the wine is corrupted, the Sacrament is not confected."],"defectus-iv-2":["Of a defect of the wine","2. If it has begun to sour or corrupt, or is somewhat sharp, or is must just pressed from the grapes, or no water has been mixed with it, or rose-water or another distilled water has been mixed with it, the Sacrament is confected, but he who confects it sins gravely."],"defectus-iv-3":["Of a defect of the wine","3. If before the consecration of the Blood, though after that of the Body, the celebrant notices that either the wine or the water or both are not in the chalice, he must at once put in wine with water, and having made the offering as above, consecrate beginning from the words <b class=\"rb-inc\">Simili modo</b>."],"defectus-iv-4":["Of a defect of the wine","4. If after the words of consecration he notices that not wine but water was put in, he shall pour the water into some vessel, put wine with water in the chalice again, and consecrate, taking up again from the said words <b class=\"rb-inc\">Simili modo</b>."],"defectus-iv-5":["Of a defect of the wine","5. If he notices this after consuming the Body, or such water, he shall set out another host to be consecrated again, and wine with water in the chalice; he shall offer both, consecrate and consume them, though he is not fasting. Or, if the Mass is celebrated in a public place where many are present, to avoid scandal he may put in wine with water, and having made the offering as above, consecrate it, consume it at once, and continue the rest."],"defectus-iv-6":["Of a defect of the wine","6. If anyone perceives before or after the consecration that all the wine is vinegar, or otherwise corrupt, the same is to be observed as above, as if he found that no wine had been put in, or only water had been put in the chalice."],"defectus-iv-7":["Of a defect of the wine","7. If the celebrant notices before the consecration of the chalice that water was not put in, he shall put it in at once and say the words of consecration. If he notices it after the consecration of the chalice, he shall by no means put it in, for it is not necessary to the Sacrament."],"defectus-iv-8":["Of a defect of the wine","8. If the matter that should be set out because of a defect of bread or wine cannot be had in any way: if this is before the consecration of the Body, he must not go further; if after the consecration of the Body, or even of the wine, a defect of the other species is found, the one being already consecrated, then, if it can in no way be had, he shall proceed and finish the Mass, but leaving out the words and signs which belong to the lacking species. But if by waiting a while it can be had, he shall wait, lest the sacrifice remain incomplete."],"defectus-v-1":["Of defects of the form","1. Defects on the part of the form may occur if anything is lacking of what is required for the integrity of the words in the consecration itself. The words of consecration, which are the form of this Sacrament, are these: Hoc est enim Corpus meum. And: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Hic est enim calix Sanguinis mei</b>, novi et aeterni testamenti, mysterium fidei, qui pro vobis, et pro multis effundetur in remissionem peccatorum. If anyone shortened or changed anything in the form of the consecration of the Body and Blood, and in that change of words the words did not mean the same thing, he would not confect the Sacrament. If anyone added something that did not change the meaning, he would confect it, but would sin most gravely."],"defectus-v-2":["Of defects of the form","2. If the celebrant does not remember having said what is ordinarily said in the consecration, he should not be troubled on that account. If, however, he is certain that he has left out something necessary to the Sacrament, that is, the form of consecration or part of it, he shall take up the form again and continue the rest in order."],"defectus-v-3":["Of defects of the form","3. If he doubts with very good reason that he has left out something essential, he shall repeat the form, at least under a tacit condition. But if the things are not necessary to the Sacrament, he shall not take them up again, but go on."],"defectus-vi-1":["Of defects of the minister","Defects on the part of the minister may occur as to what is required in him. These are: first, the intention; then the disposition of soul, the disposition of body, the disposition of the vestments, and the disposition in the ministry itself as to what may occur in it."],"defectus-vii-1":["Of a defect of intention","1. If anyone does not intend to confect, but to do something in mockery."],"defectus-vii-2":["Of a defect of intention","2. Likewise, if some hosts remain on the altar through forgetfulness, or some part of the wine, or some host lies hidden, they are not consecrated, since he intends to consecrate only those he sees."],"defectus-vii-3":["Of a defect of intention","3. Likewise, if anyone has eleven hosts before him and intends to consecrate only ten, without determining which ten he intends, he does not consecrate, for a determinate intention is required."],"defectus-vii-4":["Of a defect of intention","4. It is otherwise if, thinking there were ten, he nevertheless willed to consecrate all that he had before him: for then all will be consecrated. And therefore every priest should always have such an intention, namely of consecrating all that he has set before him to be consecrated."],"defectus-vii-5":["Of a defect of intention","5. If a priest, thinking he holds one host, finds after the consecration that there were two joined together, he shall consume both together at the Communion."],"defectus-vii-6":["Of a defect of intention","6. If after consuming the Body and Blood, or even after the ablution, he finds some consecrated remnants left, he shall consume them, whether small or large, for they belong to the same sacrifice."],"defectus-vii-7":["Of a defect of intention","7. If a whole consecrated host has been left, he shall put it in the tabernacle with the others; if this cannot be done, he shall leave it for the next priest who is to celebrate there, decently covered on the corporal on the altar, to be consumed together with the one he will consecrate; or if neither can be done, he shall keep it decently in the chalice or on the paten until it is put in the tabernacle or consumed by another; and if he has no way of keeping it fittingly, he may consume it himself."],"defectus-vii-8":["Of a defect of intention","8. If the intention is not actual at the consecration itself because of wandering of mind, but is virtual, since in coming to the altar he intends to do what the Church does, the Sacrament is confected; though the priest should take care to have an actual intention as well."],"defectus-viii-1":["Of defects of the disposition of soul","1. If anyone who is suspended, excommunicated, degraded, irregular or otherwise canonically impeded celebrates, he confects the Sacrament, but sins most gravely, both because of the Communion which he receives unworthily and because of the exercise of Orders which was forbidden him."],"defectus-viii-2":["Of defects of the disposition of soul","2. If anyone who has access to a confessor celebrates in mortal sin, he sins gravely."],"defectus-viii-3":["Of defects of the disposition of soul","3. If anyone, even in a case of necessity, not having access to a confessor, celebrates in mortal sin without contrition, he sins gravely."],"defectus-viii-4":["Of defects of the disposition of soul","4. It is otherwise if he is contrite; but he must confess as soon as he can."],"defectus-viii-5":["Of defects of the disposition of soul","5. If during the very celebration of Mass the priest remembers that he is in mortal sin, he shall make an act of contrition with the purpose of confessing and making satisfaction. If he remembers that he is excommunicated or suspended, or that the place is under interdict, he shall likewise make an act of contrition with the purpose of seeking absolution; but before the consecration, in the cases mentioned, if no scandal is feared, he must leave the Mass he has begun."],"defectus-ix-1":["Of defects of the disposition of body","1. If anyone is not fasting from midnight, even after taking water alone, or any other drink or food, even by way of medicine and in however small a quantity, he may neither communicate nor celebrate."],"defectus-ix-2":["Of defects of the disposition of body","2. But if he took food or drink before midnight, even if he has not slept since and it is not digested, he does not sin; but because of the disturbance of mind by which devotion is taken away, it is sometimes advised to abstain."],"defectus-ix-3":["Of defects of the disposition of body","3. If remnants of food remaining in the mouth are swallowed, they do not prevent Communion, since they are swallowed not as food but as saliva. The same is to be said if, in washing the mouth, a drop of water is swallowed unintentionally."],"defectus-ix-4":["Of defects of the disposition of body","4. If he celebrates several Masses on one day, as on the Nativity of the Lord, at each Mass he shall wash his fingers over some fitting vessel, and take the ablution of the fingers only at the last."],"defectus-ix-5":["Of defects of the disposition of body","5. If a nocturnal pollution has occurred which was caused by a preceding thought that was a mortal sin, or happened through excess of food and drink, one must abstain from Communion and from celebrating, unless the confessor judges otherwise. If it is doubtful whether there was mortal sin in the preceding thought, it is advised to abstain, except in a case of necessity. But if it is certain that there was no mortal sin in that thought, or that there was no evil thought at all, but that it happened from a natural cause or from an illusion of the devil, he may communicate and celebrate, unless such disturbance of mind has come from that bodily disturbance that it seems he should abstain."],"defectus-x-1":["Of defects occurring in the ministry itself","Defects may also occur in the ministry itself, if anything required for it is lacking; and this may come about in several ways."],"defectus-x-2":["Of defects occurring in the ministry itself","Of defects in the circumstances of place and time. 1. If Mass is celebrated in a place not sacred, or not designated by the Bishop, or by an Abbot having the faculty."],"defectus-x-3":["Of defects occurring in the ministry itself","2. If it is celebrated in a church under interdict or violated. If the church is violated while the priest is celebrating, before the Canon, the Mass shall be abandoned; if after the Canon, it shall not be abandoned."],"defectus-x-4":["Of defects occurring in the ministry itself","3. If an attack of enemies, or a flood, or the collapse of the place where Mass is celebrated is feared: before the consecration the Mass shall be abandoned; after the consecration the priest may hasten the consumption of the Sacrament, leaving out everything else."],"defectus-x-5":["Of defects occurring in the ministry itself","4. If it is not the due time for celebrating, which is ordinarily from dawn to midday."],"defectus-xi-1":["Of defects in what is required on the part of the priest and of the altar","1. If the priest celebrates without having said Matins with Lauds."],"defectus-xi-2":["Of defects in what is required on the part of the priest and of the altar","2. If he celebrates with his head covered without dispensation."],"defectus-xi-3":["Of defects in what is required on the part of the priest and of the altar","3. If he leaves off any of the priestly vestments."],"defectus-xi-4":["Of defects in what is required on the part of the priest and of the altar","4. If the priestly vestments and the altar cloths are not blessed by the Bishop, or by another having this power."],"defectus-xi-5":["Of defects in what is required on the part of the priest and of the altar","5. If the palls, purificators and corporals are not clean, and blessed by the Bishop or by another having this power."],"defectus-xi-6":["Of defects in what is required on the part of the priest and of the altar","6. If he celebrates on an altar not consecrated, or not covered with three cloths."],"defectus-xi-7":["Of defects in what is required on the part of the priest and of the altar","7. If there is not a fitting consecrated chalice with its paten, whose cup must be of gold, silver or tin, not of bronze or glass."],"defectus-xi-8":["Of defects in what is required on the part of the priest and of the altar","8. If there is no Missal, even though he knows by heart the Mass he intends to say."],"defectus-xi-9":["Of defects in what is required on the part of the priest and of the altar","9. If there are no wax candles."],"defectus-xii-1":["Of the lack of a server","If there is no cleric or other person to serve the Mass, or one is present who ought not to serve, such as a woman."],"defectus-xiii-1":["Of the failing or illness of the priest","1. If before the consecration the priest falls gravely ill, or faints, or dies, the Mass is left off; if this happens after the consecration of the Body only, before that of the Blood, or after both have been consecrated, the Mass shall be completed by another priest from the place where he left off, and in case of necessity even by one not fasting."],"defectus-xiii-2":["Of the failing or illness of the priest","2. If he has not died, but is ill, yet so that he can communicate, and no other consecrated host is at hand, the priest who completes the Mass shall divide the host, give one part to the sick priest, and consume the other himself."],"defectus-xiii-3":["Of the failing or illness of the priest","3. If the priest dies when the form of the Body is half spoken, since the consecration has not been made, it is not necessary that the Mass be completed by another."],"defectus-xiii-4":["Of the failing or illness of the priest","4. But if he dies when the form of the Blood is half spoken, another shall likewise continue the Mass and repeat the whole form over the same chalice, from the words <b class=\"rb-inc\">Simili modo</b> postquam cenatum est; or he must say the whole form over another chalice made ready, consume the host of the first priest and the Blood he has consecrated himself, and then the half-consecrated chalice that was left."],"defectus-xiii-5":["Of the failing or illness of the priest","5. If anyone, outside such cases of necessity, does not consume the Sacrament entire, he sins most gravely."],"defectus-xiv-1":["Of something harmful falling into the chalice or on the host","1. If a fly, a spider or anything else falls into the chalice before the consecration, he shall pour the wine out under the altar or in some other fitting place, put other wine in the chalice, mix in a little water, offer it as above, and continue the Mass."],"defectus-xiv-2":["Of something harmful falling into the chalice or on the host","2. If a fly or the like falls in after the consecration, and the priest feels nausea, he shall take it out and wash it with wine; when Mass is finished he shall burn it, and the ashes and the washing shall be thrown into the sacrarium. But if he feels no nausea and fears no danger, he shall consume it with the Blood."],"defectus-xiv-3":["Of something harmful falling into the chalice or on the host","3. If something poisonous, or something that would cause vomiting, falls into the chalice, the consecrated wine is to be put in another chalice, and other wine with water set out to be consecrated anew; and when Mass is finished the Blood so put aside shall be kept in a linen cloth or tow until the species of wine have dried; then the tow shall be burnt and the ashes thrown into the sacrarium."],"defectus-xiv-4":["Of something harmful falling into the chalice or on the host","4. If something poisonous has touched the consecrated host, he shall consecrate another, having made the offering as above, and consume it in the way described; and the first shall be kept in the tabernacle in a separate place until the species corrupt, and once corrupted they shall be put into the sacrarium; and the animal, if caught, shall be killed and burnt, as was said above."],"defectus-xv-1":["Of the breaking of the host and the freezing of the Blood","1. If the host is found broken before the consecration, unless this is evident to the people, such a host shall be consecrated."],"defectus-xv-2":["Of the breaking of the host and the freezing of the Blood","2. But if it could be a scandal to the people, another shall be taken and offered; and if the offering of that host had already been made, he shall consume it after the ablution."],"defectus-xv-3":["Of the breaking of the host and the freezing of the Blood","3. If before the offering the host appears broken, another whole one shall be taken, if this can be done without scandal or long delay."],"defectus-xv-4":["Of the breaking of the host and the freezing of the Blood","4. If through cold or carelessness the consecrated host slips into the chalice, nothing is to be repeated on that account; but the priest shall continue the Mass, leaving out the signs and ceremonies usually made with it, and shall consume the Body together with the Blood, saying Corpus, et Sanguis."],"defectus-xv-5":["Of the breaking of the host and the freezing of the Blood","5. If in consuming the Blood a particle remains in the chalice, he shall bring it with his finger to the lip of the chalice and consume it before the purification, or pour in wine and consume it."],"defectus-xv-6":["Of the breaking of the host and the freezing of the Blood","6. If in winter the Blood freezes in the chalice, the chalice shall be wrapped in warmed cloths; if that does not help, it shall be set in hot water near the altar, provided the water does not get into the chalice, until it melts."],"defectus-xvi-1":["Of the spilling of the Blood or the falling of the host","1. If through carelessness some of the Blood of Christ falls: if on the ground or on a board, it shall be taken up with the tongue, and the place scraped as much as is needed, and the scrapings burnt, the ashes being put into the sacrarium."],"defectus-xvi-2":["Of the spilling of the Blood or the falling of the host","2. If it falls on the altar stone, the priest shall take up the drop, and the place shall be well washed, and the washing thrown into the sacrarium."],"defectus-xvi-3":["Of the spilling of the Blood or the falling of the host","3. If it falls on the altar cloth and the drop reaches another cloth, or as far as the third, the cloths shall be washed three times where the drop fell, with the chalice held beneath, and the water of the washing thrown into the sacrarium."],"defectus-xvi-4":["Of the spilling of the Blood or the falling of the host","4. If it falls on the corporal alone, or on the priestly vestments, they must likewise be washed, and the washing thrown into the sacrarium."],"defectus-xvi-5":["Of the spilling of the Blood or the falling of the host","5. If on the cloth or carpet beneath the feet, it must be washed as above."],"defectus-xvi-6":["Of the spilling of the Blood or the falling of the host","6. If it should happen that the whole of the Blood is spilt after the consecration: if anything, even a little, remains, it shall be consumed, and with the rest of the Blood that was spilt it shall be done as said above."],"defectus-xvi-7":["Of the spilling of the Blood or the falling of the host","7. If nothing at all remains, he shall put in wine and water again and consecrate from the words <b class=\"rb-inc\">Simili modo</b> postquam cenatum est, having first made the offering of the chalice, at least mentally, as said above."],"defectus-xvi-8":["Of the spilling of the Blood or the falling of the host","8. If the priest vomits the Eucharist: if the species appear whole, they shall be reverently consumed, unless nausea arises; for then the consecrated species shall be carefully separated and put in some sacred place until they corrupt, and afterwards put into the sacrarium."],"defectus-xvi-9":["Of the spilling of the Blood or the falling of the host","9. If the species do not appear, the vomit shall be burnt and the ashes thrown into the sacrarium."],"defectus-xvi-10":["Of the spilling of the Blood or the falling of the host","10. If the consecrated host, or any particle of it, falls to the ground, it shall be reverently taken up; and the place where it fell cleaned and scraped a little, and the dust or scrapings thrown into the sacrarium."],"defectus-xvi-11":["Of the spilling of the Blood or the falling of the host","11. If it falls outside the corporal onto the altar cloth, or in any other way onto some linen, the cloth or linen shall be carefully washed, and the washing poured into the sacrarium."],"defectus-xvi-12":["Of the spilling of the Blood or the falling of the host","Defects may also occur in the ministry itself if the priest does not know the rites and ceremonies to be observed in it, of all of which it will be fully spoken in the following rubrics."],"praeparatio-i-1":["The preparation","When he wishes to celebrate Mass, he shall first consult his conscience, considering whether he has offended God or his neighbour in anything. Then, after due preparation, he shall confess his sins before putting on the sacred vestments, the confessor sitting and he kneeling with head uncovered, so that he may come clean to the altar; and having done his penance, he shall give himself a little to prayer, saying the prayers set down below as time allows."],"praeparatio-ii-1":["What he is to do in the sacristy","When the priest who is to celebrate comes to the sacristy, Matins at least being finished, having called upon the divine Name, which he needs in this work beyond doubt more than in any other, he shall take the Missal, find the Mass, read it through, and set the markers at what he is to say. Then he shall carefully wash his hands and face, saying: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Da Domine virtutem manibus meis ad abstergendam omnem maculam, ut sine pollutione mentis et corporis valeam tibi servire. Amen.</b> And nothing unbecoming, unseemly or untidy shall appear to the eyes of those coming to hear Mass."],"praeparatio-ii-2":["What he is to do in the sacristy","Then he shall prepare the chalice, carefully wiping it with a clean purificator kept for this. He shall lay the purificator over its mouth, and on it the paten with the host, which he shall carefully clean of every roughness, so that it has nothing that might be judged unworthy of the sacrifice, being neither broken, nor spotted, nor pierced with holes. He shall cover it with the small pall and a silk veil, and on the veil set the burse of the colour of the vestments, with the corporal folded inside it, and draw the front of the veil back over the burse."],"praeparatio-ii-3":["What he is to do in the sacristy","These things being arranged, coming to put on the sacred vestments, he shall be shod, and clothed in all the white habit of the Order, and signing himself with the cross he shall say: <b class=\"rb-inc\">In nomine Patris, et Filii, et Spiritus Sancti. Amen</b>. Then, taking the amice by its ends and strings, he shall kiss the cross in the middle and, putting it on his head, say: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Impone Domine capiti meo galeam salutis, ad expugnandos omnes diabolicos incursus. Amen.</b> With it he shall cover the capuce all round, and drawing the strings crosswise under the arms, fasten them at the cincture; or, if they are long, draw them round the back, bring them before the breast and tie them, speaking with God in prayer and in meditation on the most holy Passion of Christ."],"praeparatio-ii-4":["What he is to do in the sacristy","Then, putting on the alb, he shall say: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dealba me Domine, et munda cor meum, ut in sanguine Agni dealbatus, gaudiis perfruar sempiternis. Amen.</b> Girding himself with the cincture, he shall let only its two ends hang down, and say: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Praecinge me Domine cingulo puritatis, et exstingue in lumbis meis humorem libidinis, ut maneat in me virtus continentiae et castitatis. Amen.</b> The server shall draw up the alb, if too long, all round over the cincture, so that it hangs becomingly and covers the habit, and carefully arrange its hem so that it falls evenly about a finger&#x27;s breadth from the ground."],"praeparatio-ii-5":["What he is to do in the sacristy","Then, kissing the maniple in the middle, where the cross should be, he shall put it on his left arm, saying: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Merear Domine portare manipulum fletus et doloris, ut cum exsultatione recipiam mercedem laboris. Amen.</b> Next he shall put the stole on his neck, kissing its middle in the same way, and saying: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Redde mihi Domine, obsecro, stolam immortalitatis, quam perdidi in praevaricatione primi parentis, et quamvis indignus accedo ad tuum sacrum mysterium, merear tamen gaudium sempiternum. Amen.</b> And crossing it before the breast, he shall bring the part hanging from the left shoulder to the right, and the part hanging from the right shoulder to the left, and so fasten the stole with the hanging ends of the cincture on either side. Lastly he shall put on the chasuble becomingly, saying: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Domine, qui dixisti: Jugum meum suave est, et onus meum leve: fac ut istud portare sic valeam, quod consequar tuam gratiam. Amen.</b>"],"praeparatio-ii-6":["What he is to do in the sacristy","Then, taking the chalice in his left hand by the knot below the cup, he shall hold with the other hand the burse of the corporal, with the veil drawn back, and the paten, so that the thumb touches the chalice and the other fingers, laid above, hold the corporal and the paten. And so, bowing his head in reverence to the cross or image that is in the sacristy, he shall go to the altar with his head covered by the capuce and amice, eyes cast down, with grave step and upright body, praying to God with sighs."],"praeparatio-ii-7":["What he is to do in the sacristy","If meanwhile he happens to pass before the tabernacle of the most holy Body of Christ, he shall genuflect on the right knee, his head covered as said. If he happens to pass an altar where Mass is being celebrated at the elevation, he shall kneel on both knees and, uncovering his head, adore, and not rise before the celebrant has put the chalice down on the corporal. If the Sacrament is openly exposed on an altar, or is being given from it, he shall likewise kneel on both knees and, uncovering his head, adore; and rising at once, go on in the way described."],"praeparatio-ii-8":["What he is to do in the sacristy","When he comes to the altar, having made a profound bow at its middle without bending the knees, or a genuflexion on one knee if the tabernacle of the most holy Sacrament is on it, or on both if the Sacrament is exposed, he shall go up to the middle of the altar, and there, before setting down the chalice, at once put it aside towards the Gospel corner, to leave room for unfolding the corporal, which he takes from the burse and reverently unfolds in the middle of the altar. Then, removing the veil and pall, he shall lay the paten with the host on the corporal; and taking the purificator from the chalice, spread it lengthwise beside the right side of the corporal."],"praeparatio-ii-9":["What he is to do in the sacristy","Then, to prepare the chalice, holding it by the middle of the knot with his left hand, and taking the cruet of wine from the server with his right, he shall pour in a little wine; then, the server holding out the cruet of water and saying <b class=\"rb-inc\">Benedicite</b>, he shall sign it with the cross, saying: <b class=\"rb-inc\">In nomine Patris, et Filii, et Spiritus Sancti</b>; and the server answers: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Amen</b>. Then he shall let fall into the chalice so little water that it can very easily be wholly changed into wine; then wipe the chalice with the purificator; and the chalice thus prepared, he shall put on it the paten with the host, cover it with the pall and veil, and set it on the corporal in the middle of the altar; and he shall go to the Missal, made ready on the cushion at the Epistle corner, open it and find the Mass."],"praeparatio-ii-10":["What he is to do in the sacristy","This done, he shall return with hands joined before the breast to the middle of the altar, and there uncover his head with both hands and, arranging the capuce becomingly, pray secretly and humbly: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Actiones nostras, quaesumus Domine, aspirando praeveni, et adjuvando prosequere: ut cuncta nostra operatio a te semper incipiat, et per te coepta finiatur. Per Christum Dominum nostrum. Amen.</b> Then he shall go on attentively and devoutly to the celebration of Mass."],"modus-p-1":["The manner and rite of saying Mass","The Missal takes the Mass of the Epiphany as its example; the text of the prayers is marked with letters, and the rubrics answer to the same letters. Here each rubric is set out with the words of the prayer to which it belongs."],"modus-i-1":["At the beginning of Mass","The priest, with hands joined before his breast, standing immediately below the top step (or below the step, if there is only one) before the middle of the altar, having made a profound bow, begins in a clear and audible voice: <b class=\"rb-inc\">In nomine Patris, et Filii, et Spiritus Sancti. Amen</b>, signing himself with his right hand from the forehead to the breast and from the left shoulder to the right."],"modus-i-2":["At the beginning of Mass","With hands joined he says in the same voice: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Confitemini Domino quoniam bonus</b>. The server answers: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Quoniam in saeculum misericordia ejus</b>. Bowing profoundly, with hands joined before his breast, he says in a moderate voice: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Confiteor</b>. He does not strike his breast at <b class=\"rb-inc\">mea culpa</b>. The server says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Misereatur tui</b>. The priest answers: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Amen</b>. The server says the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Confiteor</b> (with <b class=\"rb-inc\">et tibi, Pater</b>); he does not strike his breast."],"modus-i-3":["At the beginning of Mass","The priest says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Misereatur vestri</b>. The server answers: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Amen</b>. The priest says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Absolutionem</b>; he does not make the sign of the cross. The server answers: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Amen</b>. Standing fully erect, with hands joined before his breast, he says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Adjutorium nostrum in nomine Domini</b>, and so goes up to the altar. The server answers: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Qui fecit caelum et terram</b>."],"modus-i-4":["At the beginning of Mass","Bowing profoundly straight towards the altar, with hands joined, he says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Aufer a nobis</b>. At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per Christum Dominum nostrum</b>, making a cross with his thumb in the middle of the altar, he kisses it, his hands extended on either side and laid on the altar. Standing erect before the middle of the altar, signing himself as above, he says secretly: <b class=\"rb-inc\">In nomine Patris, et Filii, et Spiritus Sancti. Amen</b>; then, with hands joined as before, he goes to the book at the Epistle corner."],"modus-ii-1":["At the Introit, Kyrie and Gloria","In a clear and audible voice he begins the Introit. At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria Patri</b>, bowing his head a little towards the cross, he shows reverence. Then he repeats the Introit up to the verse exclusively. He says the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Kyrie eleison</b>, etc., alternately with the server."],"modus-ii-2":["At the Introit, Kyrie and Gloria","The last Kyrie said, if the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria</b> is to be said he goes with hands joined to the middle of the altar; if not, he stays there before the book and continues the rest. At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria</b> he lays his extended hands on the altar; at <b class=\"rb-inc\">in excelsis</b> they are raised; at Deo they are joined; and bowing his head, he returns to the Epistle corner and there continues the rest reading from the book, with hands joined before his breast. At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Adoramus te</b>, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gratias agimus tibi</b>, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Jesu Christe, suscipe deprecationem nostram</b>, and again at <b class=\"rb-inc\">Jesu Christe</b>, he bows his head."],"modus-iii-1":["At the Collects","In the same place he turns to the people from the left side to the right of his body, that is, by the side towards the Epistle corner (except when he celebrates on an altar on which is the Sacrament of the Holy Eucharist), with his hands straight, raised so that they do not go above the shoulders, and extended so that they may be seen from behind; this is to be observed whenever the hands are raised and extended, except at <b class=\"rb-inc\">Unde et memores</b>. He says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b>, and joins his hands. The server answers: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Et cum spiritu tuo</b>."],"modus-iii-2":["At the Collects","Then, returning to the book by the same side, with hands joined, bowing his head a little towards the cross of the altar, he says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Oremus</b>. His hands are opened for the Collect; and when other commemorations are to be said, he does at the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Oremus</b> and at the prayer as just described. At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per Dominum nostrum</b> he joins his hands and shows reverence to the Name of God, bowing his head a little towards the cross of the altar."],"modus-iii-3":["At the Collects","If after <b class=\"rb-inc\">Oremus</b> the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Flectamus genua</b> is to be said before the Collects (the server answering <b class=\"rb-inc\">Levate</b>), he takes the front of the chasuble on either side with both hands and, laying it on the altar, kneels, and rising at once says the Collect as above."],"modus-iv-1":["From the Epistle to the Gospel","With his hands crossed upon the book, or resting on the altar, he reads the Epistle or Lesson. At the Gradual he raises his hands, and keeps them joined until the Gospel is pronounced. With hands joined before his breast he says the Gradual, the Alleluia or Tract, and the Sequence."],"modus-iv-2":["From the Epistle to the Gospel","When the Alleluia or Tract is finished, the server carries the Missal to the Gospel corner, and the priest goes there with hands joined, showing reverence to the cross in the middle of the altar and saying secretly: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus sit in corde meo, et in labiis meis, ad pronuntiandum sanctum Evangelium pacis</b>. The server places the Missal so that its top faces the corner of the altar."],"modus-v-1":["At the Gospel","He lays his joined hands becomingly on the Missal and says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b>. The server answers: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Et cum spiritu tuo</b>. At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Sequentia sancti Evangelii</b> he signs the Gospel with the cross with the thumb of his right hand. The server answers: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria tibi Domine</b>. With small crosses of the thumb he signs his forehead, mouth and breast; then with a larger sign of the cross with his right hand he signs his whole person, from forehead to breast and shoulders."],"modus-v-2":["At the Gospel","He lays his joined hands again on the Missal and so continues the whole Gospel, except when he says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Et procidentes adoraverunt eum</b>; for then he genuflects towards the book, which he is always to do when there is a genuflexion in the Gospel; and when he says the name of Jesus or of Mary he bows his head."],"modus-v-3":["At the Gospel","At the end of the Gospel he signs himself again with the larger sign of the cross. Then, signing the Gospel with the cross, he says secretly: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per Evangelica dicta deleantur nostra delicta</b>, kissing the Gospel, except in Masses for the Dead and when the Passions are read."],"modus-vi-1":["At the Credo","At the middle of the altar, his hands laid extended on the altar, he says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Credo</b>; at <b class=\"rb-inc\">in unum</b> he raises his hands; at Deum he joins them and bows his head to the cross, and returns at once to the Gospel corner, and there continues the rest in the book with hands joined before his breast. At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Jesum Christum</b> he bows his head towards the book."],"modus-vi-2":["At the Credo","At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Et incarnatus est</b> he comes to the middle of the altar and kneels, and having said <b class=\"rb-inc\">Homo factus est</b> he rises, joins his hands as before, and returns at once to the book to read the rest; at <b class=\"rb-inc\">simul adoratur</b> he bows his head; at <b class=\"rb-inc\">Et vitam venturi saeculi</b> he signs himself with the cross from forehead to breast and shoulders."],"modus-vii-1":["At the Offertory","And coming to the middle of the altar and turning to the people, opening and joining his hands as at the first, he says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b>, and returns to the middle by the same way. The server answers: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Et cum spiritu tuo</b>. With hands opened and raised, bowing his head to the cross, he begins: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Oremus</b>, and at the last syllable joins them before his breast."],"modus-vii-2":["At the Offertory","With hands joined he goes to the book, and having said the Offertory, bringing the book nearer, he returns to the middle of the altar; with his left hand and his right he lifts the veil with the pall from the chalice and sets it aside on the Epistle side; then he lays his hands on the altar and says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Quid retribuam</b>, raising them, and at <b class=\"rb-inc\">Calicem salutaris</b> joining them."],"modus-vii-3":["At the Offertory","At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Suscipe sancta Trinitas</b> he takes the chalice, with the paten and the host upon it, in both hands, and raising it above the corporal to the height of his breast, holding the middle of the chalice with the right hand and the foot with the left, he says the prayer with his eyes raised to God. With his right hand he sets the chalice down on the corporal in the middle; and at once with his left he lifts the paten from the chalice, and, putting his right hand also to the paten, lets the host down before the foot of the chalice so that it does not touch it. He puts the paten back at the right of the corporal, half over the purificator and under the corporal. Then he covers the chalice with the pall."],"modus-vii-4":["At the Offertory","With hands joined at once, and bowing his head to the cross, he goes to the Epistle corner to wash his hands, saying: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Lavabo inter innocentes</b>... <b class=\"rb-inc\">Domine dilexi decorem domus tuae, et locum habitationis gloriae tuae</b>; and no further. Having washed the first four fingers and dried them, he returns with hands joined before his breast to the middle of the altar; when he has arrived there, he begins <b class=\"rb-inc\">In spiritu humilitatis</b>, bowing profoundly to the altar, and at <b class=\"rb-inc\">et placeat tibi</b> he stands erect. With the fingers with which he is to handle the Body of the Lord he is not to turn the pages or touch anything."],"modus-vii-5":["At the Offertory","Turned to the people, with hands raised and extended, he says in a moderate voice: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Orate fratres</b>. With hands joined before his breast, continuing secretly: <b class=\"rb-inc\">ut meum, ac vestrum pariter in conspectu Domini sit acceptum sacrificium</b>, he completes the circle towards the Gospel corner and returns to the Missal. With hands joined as before he says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Domine exaudi orationem meam, et clamor meus ad te veniat</b>. With hands likewise joined he says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Oremus</b>; with them opened and extended as above, he says the Secret; and at Per Dominum he joins them, bowing his head a little; and if other commemorations are to be made, he again says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Oremus</b> before the first of them, with hands joined, opening them for the prayer."],"modus-viii-1":["At the Preface","These finished, standing in the middle of the altar with his hands laid on the altar on either side, he begins in a clear and audible voice: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per omnia saecula saeculorum</b>. The server answers: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Amen</b>. With his hands laid down as before, he says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b>. The server answers: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Et cum spiritu tuo</b>. At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Sursum corda</b> he raises his hands and extends them. The server answers: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Habemus ad Dominum</b>. At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gratias agamus</b> he joins his hands, and at <b class=\"rb-inc\">Deo nostro</b>, his head bowed to the cross, he afterwards opens them. The server answers: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dignum et justum est</b>."],"modus-viii-2":["At the Preface","With hands extended and raised in the usual way, he says the common or proper Preface as the day requires. At <b class=\"rb-inc\">supplici confessione dicentes</b> he joins his hands before his breast. With hands joined as before he continues in the same voice: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Sanctus, Sanctus, Sanctus</b>. At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Benedictus, qui venit</b> he signs himself with the cross from forehead to breast and shoulders."],"modus-ix-1":["At the Canon","With hands joined before his breast and bowing profoundly, he says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Te igitur</b>. At uti he stands erect, and at benedicas he lays his left hand on the altar and with his right signs the whole sacrifice with three crosses, as marked, that is with only the first three fingers extended and the others bent towards the palm; and at <b class=\"rb-inc\">In primis</b> he continues with hands joined before his breast; and at Papa he says the proper name of the Pope (when the See is vacant these words are left out). At Antistite the proper name of the Bishop is said, in his own diocese, and not that of any other superior; if he is dead, or when Mass is celebrated in Rome, these words are left out. At Rege the name is said in the dominions of a king."],"modus-ix-2":["At the Canon","Likewise with hands joined before his breast he says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Memento Domine</b>. After tuarum, with hands joined as before and eyes lowered, he remembers for a short while the living whom he wishes to commend to God, in this way: his father, mother, brothers, sisters, kindred, benefactors, superiors, and according to the intention of all those for whom he is bound to pray; and this is done not in words but in the mind only; then he continues the rest."],"modus-ix-3":["At the Canon","He says the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Communicantes</b> with hands extended and raised in the usual way, and bows his head at the names of Mary and of Jesus. At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per eumdem Christum</b> he joins his hands and bows his head. Extending and raising his hands he says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Hanc igitur</b>. And so he continues the rest."],"modus-ix-4":["At the Canon","At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Quam oblationem</b>, his left hand laid on the altar, with the first three fingers of the right extended as above, he makes the crosses: the first three over the whole sacrifice, and the other two, one at Corpus over the Host only, and the other at Sanguis over the chalice only. At Jesu Christi he bows his head."],"modus-x-1":["At the consecration","At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Qui pridie</b> he wipes the tips of the thumbs and forefingers of both hands over the corporal; with the forefinger of the left hand he presses on the Host so that, the other side being raised, it can be taken by the right, holding it with the forefinger and thumb of each hand; at <b class=\"rb-inc\">elevatis oculis in caelum</b> he raises his eyes to heaven and at once lowers them; and at <b class=\"rb-inc\">benedixit</b>, holding the Host with the left hand between thumb and forefinger, he signs it once with the cross with the first three fingers of the right extended, as above, and continues the words as far as <b class=\"rb-inc\">Accipite</b>; for then he will take the Host again with the thumb and forefinger of the right hand."],"modus-x-2":["At the consecration","Secretly, attentively, devoutly and without interruption, and with the greatest reverence, he pronounces the words of the consecration of the Body, not leaning on the altar but bending his body a little; having said them, holding the Host above the altar between the thumbs and forefingers of both hands, the other fingers extended and held together, he adores it kneeling; and rising at once, he raises it becomingly with both hands as high as he conveniently can, so that it can be seen by those standing behind, and with his eyes ever fixed on it, he offers it to the people to adore; and setting it down reverently without delay, he first withdraws his left hand and with the right places the Host on the corporal where it was before. The consecrated Host being laid on the corporal, he adores it again kneeling. After this he joins the thumb and forefinger of each hand, and does not separate them (except when he must touch or handle the consecrated Host) until after the ablution of the fingers."],"modus-x-3":["At the consecration","At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Simili modo</b> he uncovers the chalice and at once lightly shakes into the chalice the fingers of both hands with which he raised the Host. At <b class=\"rb-inc\">accipiens et hunc praeclarum Calicem</b> he takes the chalice by the middle with the right hand and by the foot with the left; at <b class=\"rb-inc\">Item tibi gratias agens</b> he raises his eyes a little and the chalice, and at once sets it down; and at <b class=\"rb-inc\">benedixit</b>, with the three last fingers of the left hand holding the foot of the chalice steady, with the three last fingers of the right he makes a cross over the chalice from lip to lip, continuing the words that follow; and at <b class=\"rb-inc\">Accipite, et bibite</b> he takes the middle of the chalice with the fingers of the right hand, the thumb and forefinger joined in front and the other three behind; and tilting it a little towards himself, he puts the thumb and forefinger of the left hand on the foot of the chalice and the other three fingers under the foot, and so holds the chalice."],"modus-x-4":["At the consecration","Standing, with head bowed, secretly, attentively, without interruption, devoutly and reverently he pronounces the words of the consecration of the Blood: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Hic est enim calix Sanguinis mei</b>. Having said them, he sets the chalice down on the corporal and, kneeling, reverently adores the Blood. Then, rising, with his eyes always on the uncovered chalice, holding it with both hands as before, he raises it for the people to adore, yet so that the foot of the chalice does not rise above the head of the priest. And setting it down on the corporal in its former place, he says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Haec quotiescumque feceritis, in mei memoriam facietis</b>, covers it with the pall with his right hand, and kneeling adores the Blood."],"modus-xi-1":["After the consecration","At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Unde et memores</b> he extends his arms more than usual, but moderately, until he must lower them to make the signs of the cross. At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Hostiam puram</b> he puts his left hand on the corporal and with the right, the three last fingers extended, makes three crosses over the whole sacrifice; and at <b class=\"rb-inc\">Panem sanctum</b> one over the Host only, and at <b class=\"rb-inc\">Calicem salutis</b> one over the chalice only."],"modus-xi-2":["After the consecration","At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Supra quae</b> he says the prayer with hands raised and extended, spreading his arms in the usual way. At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Supplices te rogamus</b> he bows profoundly, his hands crossed before his breast, the left arm beneath the right; and at <b class=\"rb-inc\">ut quotquot</b> he begins little by little to rise, so that at <b class=\"rb-inc\">altaris participatione</b> he can kiss the right side of the corporal, his arms still crossed; this done, he stands erect, puts his left hand back on the corporal, and with the right blesses the Host at Corpus and the chalice at Sanguinem, and at <b class=\"rb-inc\">benedictione caelesti</b> makes the sign of the cross before himself with the right hand from forehead to breast and shoulders (the left being placed below the breast), yet without touching himself, and not with the first three fingers with which he signed the sacrifice, but with the three last fingers, thumb and forefinger being closed; at <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per eumdem</b> he joins his hands before his breast."],"modus-xi-3":["After the consecration","With hands joined as before he says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Memento etiam</b>; and at N. N., with his eyes fixed on the sacrifice, he calls to mind the dead, as was said of the memory of the living, and then says the rest with hands opened; at <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per eumdem</b> he joins his hands. The first three words, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Nobis quoque peccatoribus</b>, he says in a clear and audible voice, lightly striking his breast with the three last fingers of the right hand; and with hands extended as before he continues the rest."],"modus-xi-4":["After the consecration","At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per quem haec omnia</b> he lays his left hand on the altar; at <b class=\"rb-inc\">sanctificas</b> he signs the whole sacrifice three times with the three last fingers of the right; he uncovers the chalice and, having said <b class=\"rb-inc\">et praestas nobis</b>, adores the Sacrament kneeling and rises at once. With the forefinger of the left hand he presses lightly on the Host so that it is raised on the other side; then he takes it with the thumb and forefinger of the right, and at once with the three last fingers of the left hand holds the foot of the chalice steady on the altar, and with the right hand holding the Host makes three crosses over the chalice at <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per ipsum</b>, the first outside from lip to lip, the second within the chalice from lip to lip, the third deeper than the second; and the fourth at <b class=\"rb-inc\">Deo Patri</b> before the lip of the chalice, somewhat raised; then he withdraws his left hand from the foot of the chalice, places it on the corporal, and at Spiritus Sancti makes a cross low down before the foot of the chalice; this done, bowing his head a little to the Host, he puts it back in its place on the corporal, but does not show it to the people; and shaking those two fingers of the right hand over the chalice, he covers it with the pall and adores the Sacrament kneeling."],"modus-xii-1":["At the Pater noster and the fraction","And laying his hands on the corporal on either side, he says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per omnia saecula saeculorum</b>. The server answers: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Amen</b>. Holding his hands in the same way he says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Oremus. Praeceptis salutaribus moniti</b>; and at <b class=\"rb-inc\">Pater noster</b> he raises his hands and extends them in the usual way. The server answers: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Sed libera nos a malo</b>. And the priest says in a low voice: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Amen</b>. In the same voice he continues: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Libera nos, quaesumus Domine</b>, with hands extended as before, and at Maria he bows his head."],"modus-xii-2":["At the Pater noster and the fraction","At <b class=\"rb-inc\">da propitius pacem</b>, his left hand laid on the corporal, he takes the paten with the right between the forefinger and middle finger and signs himself with it, touching his forehead and breast, then the left side of the breast and the right; then he kisses the paten and at once lays it on the altar apart from the corporal; and at <b class=\"rb-inc\">et ab omni perturbatione securi</b>, with the thumb and forefinger of the right hand not parted, he uncovers the chalice and adores the Sacrament kneeling; then, rising, he presses lightly on the Host with the forefinger of the left hand so that it can be taken with the forefinger and thumb of the right; having taken it, at once he holds the foot of the chalice with the three last fingers of the left hand, and with the right lifts the Host over the chalice, so that the chalice is held by the same hand; then he takes the Host with the thumb and forefinger of the left hand, and beginning <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per eumdem</b> he breaks the Host becomingly into two parts, dividing it through the middle from top to bottom, taking care that his fingers do not touch the broken edges."],"modus-xii-3":["At the Pater noster and the fraction","At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Qui tecum vivit</b> he places the half of the Host he holds in the right hand crosswise beneath the other, and from it also breaks off a particle with the thumb and forefinger of the right hand, and so, holding the parts as if joined, says in a clear voice: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per omnia saecula saeculorum</b>. The server answers: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Amen</b>. At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Pax Domini</b>, holding the two parts of the Host in the left hand over the chalice always until the Communion, and holding the other part in the right hand, he makes these three crosses with the particle of the Host within the chalice; and afterwards again holds both hands over the chalice so that the parts of the Host appear joined."],"modus-xiii-1":["At the Agnus Dei, the peace and the Communion","In a clear voice he says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Agnus Dei</b>. Then he says secretly: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Haec sacrosancta commixtio</b>, and at once reverently lets fall into the chalice the particle of the Host which he holds in the right hand, continuing the words; and having again shaken his fingers over the chalice, he at once holds the middle of the chalice with the right hand, and having said <b class=\"rb-inc\">Amen</b>, kisses the outer lip of the chalice, and giving the peace to the server, who holds the pall or the image of the peace where this is the custom, says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Pax tibi, et Ecclesiae sanctae Dei</b> (except when Mass is celebrated for the dead; for then, and in the Triduum before Easter, it is omitted, as is the kiss)."],"modus-xiii-2":["At the Agnus Dei, the peace and the Communion","With what devotion he can he says the prayer <b class=\"rb-inc\">Domine Jesu Christe, Fili Dei vivi</b>; when it is finished he commends himself to the Lord in a short mental prayer, adding no other prayer, nor does he strike his breast; but with his head only bowed, holding the chalice in his right hand, he at once takes the sacred Body of Christ into his mouth from his left, saying: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Corpus et Sanguis Domini nostri Jesu Christi custodiant me in vitam aeternam. Amen</b>. This done, and the Host consumed, he at once covers the chalice with the pall and rests a little in meditation on the most holy Sacrament; then he genuflects, and rising at once uncovers the chalice, takes the middle of the chalice with the right hand and the foot with the left, and so consumes the Blood with the particle put into the chalice, saying nothing."],"modus-xiii-3":["At the Agnus Dei, the peace and the Communion","After the first draught he lays his left hand on the altar, and so after the second; and with the right he takes the paten, with which he carefully gathers any particles of the Host there may be on the corporal, meanwhile lifting the corporal a little with the left so as to gather them more easily; then with the forefinger of the right hand he puts them into the chalice, and afterwards shakes the forefinger and thumb themselves into the chalice, lest any fragment remain on them, and consumes them, whether small or large."],"modus-xiv-1":["At the ablutions and the Postcommunion","Then, carrying the chalice in both hands, he goes to the Epistle corner for the ablution, which is poured of wine alone, saying nothing, and then consumes it. The second ablution, of wine and water, he receives over his fingers, saying secretly: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Quod ore sumpsimus</b>; then he wipes the fingers with the purificator, and afterwards, taking the chalice in the right hand, drinks it, wiping his mouth with the purificator, and lastly the chalice."],"modus-xiv-2":["At the ablutions and the Postcommunion","When it is wiped he sets the chalice on the corporal, spreading the purificator over it, putting the paten on it, and covering it with the pall and veil; and bowing his head to the cross, he goes to the Missal (which the server will have carried to the Epistle corner) and with hands joined before his breast says the Communion. Then, turning to the people, he says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b>. The server answers: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Et cum spiritu tuo</b>, as he did before the first Collect. At <b class=\"rb-inc\">Oremus</b>, with hands joined before his breast, he bows his head a little towards the cross. Then he says the Postcommunion with hands opened and extended in the usual way, and at <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per Dominum nostrum</b> joined before his breast; and at <b class=\"rb-inc\">Jesum Christum</b> he comes to the middle of the altar if it is the last prayer; otherwise, as was said, he adds the other commemorations, and at the last Per Dominum goes to the middle of the altar."],"modus-xiv-3":["At the ablutions and the Postcommunion","He says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Humiliate capita vestra Deo</b>, bowing his head, before the Prayer over the people, when the Mass is of a Lenten feria."],"modus-xv-1":["At the end of Mass","Turned to the people, with hands raised and extended, he says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b>, joining them before his breast at vobiscum. The server answers: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Et cum spiritu tuo</b>. With his face turned to the people as before, and hands joined, he says: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Ite Missa est</b>. The server answers: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Deo gratias</b>. When the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria in excelsis</b> is not said in the Mass, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Ite Missa est</b> is not said, but <b class=\"rb-inc\">Benedicamus Domino</b>, which is to be said with the face turned to the altar. And <b class=\"rb-inc\">Requiescant in pace</b> is said in the same way when the Mass is for the dead (and then the server answers <b class=\"rb-inc\">Amen</b>)."],"modus-xv-2":["At the end of Mass","Having said <b class=\"rb-inc\">Ite Missa est</b>, he turns back to the altar by the same way by which he had turned to the people; and then, bowing profoundly (even if he has said <b class=\"rb-inc\">Benedicamus Domino</b> or <b class=\"rb-inc\">Requiescant in pace</b>), with hands joined he says secretly: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Placeat tibi sancta Trinitas</b>; this said, at <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per Christum</b> he stands erect and kisses the altar. Turned to the people, he gives the blessing with the right hand fully extended, laying the left on his breast, signing the people with a single cross; and completing the circle (unless the most holy Sacrament is exposed or shut in the tabernacle) he goes to the Gospel corner."],"modus-xv-3":["At the end of Mass","In saying <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b> he signs a cross with his thumb on the altar at the place of the Gospel, or on the book. The server answers. In saying <b class=\"rb-inc\">Initium, etc.</b>, he signs his forehead, mouth and breast with the sign of the cross with his thumb, and his whole person, as was done at the first Gospel. The server answers: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria tibi Domine</b>. Meanwhile, as he continues the Gospel with hands joined, he looks towards the Gospel corner, and does not fold the corporal at that time. When he comes to <b class=\"rb-inc\">Et Verbum caro factum est</b>, he genuflects on the right knee, and at <b class=\"rb-inc\">et habitavit in nobis</b> rises. The server answers: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Deo gratias</b>; and the priest signs his whole person as above."],"modus-xv-4":["At the end of Mass","When the end of the Gospel is reached, he returns to the middle of the altar and puts the chalice aside from the corporal towards the Gospel corner; he folds the corporal, puts it folded into the burse and lays it at the Epistle corner; and having set the chalice back in the middle of the altar, he lays the burse on the veil, drawing the front of the veil over the burse as he had done when he came to Mass. Then he bows his head devoutly to the cross, and covering his head with both hands with the capuce and amice, praising God devoutly, taking the chalice by the middle with the left hand and laying the right on the burse in the manner described at the beginning of Mass, he goes down below the lowest step of the altar, and there in the middle, turned to the cross, bows profoundly (or genuflects, if the tabernacle of the most holy Sacrament is on it), and returns to the sacristy in the order in which he came, saying the canticle <b class=\"rb-inc\">Benedicite omnia opera</b>."],"varia-i-1":["Of the genuflexions in Masses both private and solemn","1. In a private Mass the priest always genuflects when he says the Gospel of St John, <b class=\"rb-inc\">In principio</b>, at the words <b class=\"rb-inc\">Et Verbum caro factum est</b>; in the Gospel of the Epiphany, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Cum natus esset Jesus</b>, at the words <b class=\"rb-inc\">Et procidentes adoraverunt eum</b>; and in the Gospel of the Wednesday after the fourth Sunday of Lent at the words at the end <b class=\"rb-inc\">Et procidens adoravit eum</b>. He also genuflects on Palm Sunday and in Masses of the Cross at the words of the Epistle <b class=\"rb-inc\">In nomine Jesu omne genu flectatur</b>. He also genuflects at the words of the Lenten Tract <b class=\"rb-inc\">Adjuva nos Deus salutaris noster</b>, and in all Masses of the Holy Ghost while he says the verse <b class=\"rb-inc\">Veni Sancte Spiritus</b>, as far as reple. Also whenever he says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Flectamus genua</b>, and when the Passion is read at the words <b class=\"rb-inc\">exspiravit</b> or <b class=\"rb-inc\">emisit spiritum</b>, and at the words of the Creed <b class=\"rb-inc\">Et incarnatus est, etc.</b>, he kneels on both knees."],"varia-i-2":["Of the genuflexions in Masses both private and solemn","2. In a solemn Mass the priest also genuflects at all the above, except at <b class=\"rb-inc\">Flectamus genua</b>, for then he alone stands, while the deacon and all the others kneel; and at the verse <b class=\"rb-inc\">Veni Sancte Spiritus</b>, if he was sitting before, he does not kneel but rises and only bows his head profoundly with the ministers. In all these, when the priest genuflects, all the ministers must genuflect too, except the acolytes holding the candlesticks at the Gospel, who then do not genuflect; and while the deacon sings the words at which one genuflects, he genuflects towards the book, the priest and all the others towards the altar."],"varia-i-3":["Of the genuflexions in Masses both private and solemn","3. Moreover he genuflects as often as the rubrics note that he should genuflect."],"varia-ii-1":["Of what is to be added in a Mass said at an altar on which the Most Holy Sacrament is exposed, or shut in the tabernacle","1. First, as far as possible one should refrain from celebrating Mass, other than a solemn Mass, before the Most Holy Sacrament, even when it is shut in the tabernacle. If necessity requires it or a just cause recommends it, the following acts of reverence and genuflexions are to be carefully observed."],"varia-ii-2":["Of what is to be added in a Mass said at an altar on which the Most Holy Sacrament is exposed, or shut in the tabernacle","2. Whenever the Most Holy Sacrament is exposed on days other than its feast and octave, a solemn Mass of the Most Holy Sacrament is to be celebrated with the due commemorations; but then the conventual Mass must also be said of the Office of the day; or one Mass is sung of the day with a commemoration of the Sacrament; and this must also be done on days on which only the proper Mass may be said and votive Masses, even solemn, are forbidden, if the Sacrament is exposed on those days."],"varia-ii-3":["Of what is to be added in a Mass said at an altar on which the Most Holy Sacrament is exposed, or shut in the tabernacle","3. On coming to the altar on which the Sacrament is exposed, all, the celebrant as well as the ministers, must kneel on both knees."],"varia-ii-4":["Of what is to be added in a Mass said at an altar on which the Most Holy Sacrament is exposed, or shut in the tabernacle","4. Each time the celebrant comes to the middle of the altar, leaves the middle, or passes before the middle, he always genuflects on one knee; and the other ministers do likewise."],"varia-ii-5":["Of what is to be added in a Mass said at an altar on which the Most Holy Sacrament is exposed, or shut in the tabernacle","5. Whenever he must turn to the community or the people to say <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b>, standing in the middle of the altar he genuflects, goes towards the Gospel corner and stands there with his back towards the corner of the altar and his face to the people, and says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b>; then by the same way, genuflecting again in the middle, he returns to the book and there says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Oremus</b> and the prayer in the usual way."],"varia-ii-6":["Of what is to be added in a Mass said at an altar on which the Most Holy Sacrament is exposed, or shut in the tabernacle","6. At the incensation what is said in the title Of the office of the ministers of the altar is to be observed, except that after incensing the chalice the priest, not standing but kneeling on both knees on the top step with the ministers duly ranged, incenses the Most Holy Sacrament, bowing his head profoundly to the Sacrament before and after the incensation. Then, standing, he will incense the altar as is said in the title cited above, genuflecting on the right knee each time he passes the middle of the altar. Also the deacon must not then kiss the priest&#x27;s hand when handing the thurible, nor when handing anything else. Also, when the priest is to be incensed, he comes down from the top step at the Epistle corner and stands there with his face to the people, and there after the incensation washes his hands. Moreover all are to take care, when they move, never to turn their backs to the Sacrament."],"varia-ii-7":["Of what is to be added in a Mass said at an altar on which the Most Holy Sacrament is exposed, or shut in the tabernacle","7. The <b class=\"rb-inc\">Orate fratres</b> and the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Ite Missa est</b> are said, and the blessing is given by the celebrant, where the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b> was said; but he does not complete the circle, but returns to the middle of the altar, where he genuflects again before the Most Holy Sacrament; and after the blessing, having genuflected, he goes to the Gospel corner to say the last Gospel."],"varia-ii-8":["Of what is to be added in a Mass said at an altar on which the Most Holy Sacrament is exposed, or shut in the tabernacle","8. The celebrant leaves the altar with the ministers, kneeling on both knees."],"varia-ii-9":["Of what is to be added in a Mass said at an altar on which the Most Holy Sacrament is exposed, or shut in the tabernacle","9. But if the Sacrament is shut in the tabernacle on the altar, whenever the celebrant says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b>, so as not to turn his back to the tabernacle, he likewise goes to the middle of the altar and, bowing his head, steps a little towards the Gospel corner, turns to the people by his right side, and by the same side, having bowed his head again before the tabernacle, returns to the book and there says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Oremus</b> and the prayer or prayers as usual."],"varia-ii-10":["Of what is to be added in a Mass said at an altar on which the Most Holy Sacrament is exposed, or shut in the tabernacle","10. He does the same at the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Orate fratres</b>, at the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Ite Missa est</b>, and while he blesses the people, noting also that then he does not complete the circle, but returns by the same way to the middle of the altar, where he bows his head again before the tabernacle; and having given the blessing he goes to the Gospel corner to say the last Gospel, as above."],"varia-ii-11":["Of what is to be added in a Mass said at an altar on which the Most Holy Sacrament is exposed, or shut in the tabernacle","11. All this is to be observed in a private Mass, apart from what is proper to the solemn Mass."],"varia-iii-1":["Of the celebration of Mass before the Supreme Pontiff, a Cardinal Legate of the Apostolic See, an Archbishop, or a Bishop in his residence","1. A priest who is to celebrate before the Supreme Pontiff stands before the lowest step of the altar on the Gospel side, before the Pontiff himself, where he waits kneeling; having received the blessing he rises, and standing turned somewhat towards the altar begins Mass. If it is before a Cardinal Legate of the Apostolic See, a Patriarch, an Archbishop or a Bishop in his residence or place of jurisdiction, he waits standing before the lowest step on the Gospel side, as above; at the sign given he makes a profound reverence to the prelate and, turned to the altar, begins Mass."],"varia-iii-2":["Of the celebration of Mass before the Supreme Pontiff, a Cardinal Legate of the Apostolic See, an Archbishop, or a Bishop in his residence","2. Celebrating solemnly before the Supreme Pontiff or another of the prelates named, in the churches of their jurisdiction, he makes the Confession with the prelate, standing at his left; and where he says <b class=\"rb-inc\">vobis fratres</b> he says <b class=\"rb-inc\">tibi Pater</b>, and likewise at the end, where he says <b class=\"rb-inc\">vos fratres</b>, he says <b class=\"rb-inc\">te Pater</b>."],"varia-iii-3":["Of the celebration of Mass before the Supreme Pontiff, a Cardinal Legate of the Apostolic See, an Archbishop, or a Bishop in his residence","3. The Confession made, if he celebrates before the Supreme Pontiff he genuflects; if before the other prelates, he bows profoundly; and going up to the middle of the altar before the lowest step, he there begins secretly <b class=\"rb-inc\">Aufer a nobis</b>."],"varia-iii-4":["Of the celebration of Mass before the Supreme Pontiff, a Cardinal Legate of the Apostolic See, an Archbishop, or a Bishop in his residence","4. After the Gospel the book of the Gospels is carried to the Supreme Pontiff or the others named to be kissed; and then the celebrant does not kiss the book, nor say <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per Evangelica dicta</b>. If several prelates are present together, the book is carried to be kissed only to the most dignified; if they are equal, to none. Nor is it carried to a Vicar Apostolic, a Commendatory or his procurator, nor to others not named in the rubric. To great secular princes (but not to lesser lay people) another book is to be carried, not the celebrant&#x27;s own, who in that case must always kiss his own book."],"varia-iii-5":["Of the celebration of Mass before the Supreme Pontiff, a Cardinal Legate of the Apostolic See, an Archbishop, or a Bishop in his residence","5. In a solemn Mass the deacon, having offered the instrument of the peace to the celebrant to kiss, goes to the prelate and the others named above and offers them the same instrument."],"varia-iii-6":["Of the celebration of Mass before the Supreme Pontiff, a Cardinal Legate of the Apostolic See, an Archbishop, or a Bishop in his residence","6. The celebrant, having said <b class=\"rb-inc\">Placeat tibi sancta Trinitas</b>, says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Benedictio Dei omnipotentis</b>, and turning to the Supreme Pontiff (when he is present) kneeling, or to a Cardinal or another of the prelates named with his head bowed, as if asking leave to bless, continues <b class=\"rb-inc\">Patris, et Filii, etc.</b>, blessing those present on the side where the Pontiff or other prelate named is not. If he has celebrated before a Patriarch, Archbishop or Bishop outside his jurisdiction, he blesses in the usual way without other regard, as for the rest who are present."],"varia-iii-7":["Of the celebration of Mass before the Supreme Pontiff, a Cardinal Legate of the Apostolic See, an Archbishop, or a Bishop in his residence","7. When the Gospel is finished, he makes a fitting reverence according to the rank of the prelates named."],"varia-iv-1":["Of what is left out in Mass for the dead","Mass for the dead is said in the manner above, except that the water is not blessed when it is poured into the chalice; and the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria Patri</b> is not said after the Introit, but after the verse <b class=\"rb-inc\">Te decet</b> the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Requiem aeternam</b> is repeated; nor is the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria in excelsis</b> said, nor <b class=\"rb-inc\">Flectamus genua</b>, nor Alleluia, nor the prayers which are only for the living, nor <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus sit in corde meo</b>, nor is the book kissed after the Gospel; the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Credo</b> is not said; the words <b class=\"rb-inc\">Quid retribuam Domino pro omnibus, quae retribuit mihi?</b> are not said; at the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Agnus Dei</b>, <b class=\"rb-inc\">miserere nobis</b> or <b class=\"rb-inc\">dona nobis pacem</b> is not said, but in its place <b class=\"rb-inc\">dona eis requiem</b>, and <b class=\"rb-inc\">sempiternam</b> is added at the last; the chalice is not kissed after <b class=\"rb-inc\">Pax Domini</b> nor is the peace given; <b class=\"rb-inc\">Humiliate capita, etc.</b> is not said; and at the end neither <b class=\"rb-inc\">Ite Missa est</b> nor <b class=\"rb-inc\">Benedicamus Domino</b> is said, but <b class=\"rb-inc\">Requiescant in pace</b>, with the face turned to the altar; nor is the blessing given; nor is any other Gospel ever said at the end than <b class=\"rb-inc\">In principio erat Verbum</b>. All else is observed as in other Masses."],"varia-v-1":["Of what is to be said in Mass aloud, in a moderate voice, or secretly","The celebrant says in a clear and audible voice: at the beginning of Mass, <b class=\"rb-inc\">In nomine Patris, et Filii, etc.</b>, and <b class=\"rb-inc\">Confitemini Domino, quoniam bonus</b>; the Introit; <b class=\"rb-inc\">Kyrie eleison</b>; <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria in excelsis</b>; <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b>, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Oremus</b>, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Flectamus genua</b>, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Levate</b>, the Collect or Collects, the Prophecies or Lessons before the Epistle; the Epistle; the Responsory, Alleluia, Tract and Sequence; the Gospel; the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Credo</b>; the Offertory; the Preface; and the three words <b class=\"rb-inc\">Nobis quoque peccatoribus</b>; also <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per omnia saecula saeculorum</b> with the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Pater noster</b>; <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per omnia saecula saeculorum</b> with the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Pax Domini</b>; the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Agnus Dei</b>, the Communion, the Postcommunion, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Humiliate capita vestra Deo</b>, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Ite Missa est</b> or <b class=\"rb-inc\">Benedicamus Domino</b> or <b class=\"rb-inc\">Requiescant in pace</b>."],"varia-v-2":["Of what is to be said in Mass aloud, in a moderate voice, or secretly","All else is said secretly, so that the priest hears only himself, except the Confession at the beginning of Mass, the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Adjutorium nostrum in nomine Domini</b>, the two words <b class=\"rb-inc\">Orate fratres</b>, and the blessing with the Gospel at the end, which are said neither secretly nor aloud, but in a moderate voice."],"varia-vi-1":["Of the servers of private Masses","1. In private Masses the brother serving wears the cappa, at least on Sundays and feasts, and always has clean hands. In his walk, his bearing, his dress and all his movements he does nothing that might offend anyone&#x27;s eye, but only what befits his holy state. He takes care that the priest&#x27;s vestments are arranged becomingly as he vests. When he hands him the amice, maniple, stole or anything else, always with his head uncovered, he reverently kisses his hand. He brings the priest the Missal, so that he may prepare the Mass before going to the altar."],"varia-vi-2":["Of the servers of private Masses","2. He spreads the altar cloths (taking off the cover) in good time and carefully, and sets out and arranges the other things about the altar. He takes care not to touch the sacred vestments or other sacred things about the altar irreverently with his own clothes. Going to the altar, carrying the Missal before his breast, he walks with religious modesty and gravity."],"varia-vi-3":["Of the servers of private Masses","3. When he has come to the altar he bows reverently, or genuflects, with the priest; then he puts the Missal on the cushion at the Epistle corner, lights the candles, and reverently ministers the wine and water, taking care that the wine is quite sound and clean, and likewise seeing that the water is fresh and clean; and he takes great care not to give the one for the other; and offering the water he says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Benedicite</b>. If he is not in major Orders, he does not touch the chalice with his bare hand, nor put in the host or the wine and water with his own hand, but holds everything out for the priest to take."],"varia-vi-4":["Of the servers of private Masses","4. At the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Confiteor Deo</b> he goes to the priest&#x27;s left and, kneeling, devoutly answers him <b class=\"rb-inc\">Misereatur tui</b>; afterwards he says the Kyrie alternately with the priest; and he does not leave that place until, the Epistle and the Alleluia with its verse having been said, or after it the Tract or Sequence, he rises, takes the book and carries it to the Gospel corner."],"varia-vi-5":["Of the servers of private Masses","5. He is to note that when the book is at the Epistle corner he must always be at the Gospel corner, and the reverse; and always kneeling, except when the Gospel is read, or when he is to minister the wine or water to the priest, light the candle for the Elevation, or carry the book across."],"varia-vi-6":["Of the servers of private Masses","6. Passing from one side to the other, he always bows reverently in the middle of the altar, or genuflects if the Most Holy Sacrament is exposed. While the priest says the Offertory he goes to the Epistle corner, unfolds and spreads out the towel and prepares the water, so as to minister it with all reverence to the priest when he comes to wash his fingers."],"varia-vi-7":["Of the servers of private Masses","7. At the Sanctus he warns those present with a few strokes of the bell; then he rises and lights the candle; at the elevation of the most holy Body and Blood of our Lord Jesus Christ, kneeling behind the priest, he rings the bell modestly, and does not fail to raise the chasuble."],"varia-vi-8":["Of the servers of private Masses","8. When the priest has communicated he rises promptly; he ministers wine for the first ablution, and wine and water for the second; he carries the book from the Gospel corner to the Epistle corner; he puts out the candle that was lit at the Elevation, and kneels at the Gospel corner."],"varia-vi-9":["Of the servers of private Masses","9. He is not to remain prostrate through the whole Mass, but to keep his eyes and mind on the altar, so as to see what is to be done and to hear when the priest is to be answered; and he must always answer him promptly and devoutly."],"varia-vi-10":["Of the servers of private Masses","10. The blessing having been given at the end of Mass, he rises and takes the book, if it is not to be carried to the Gospel corner for a Gospel to be read from it according to the day, as is said in the Rite of Mass, and puts out the candles at the proper time, so that when the Gospel that is usually said after Mass is finished he is ready to lead the priest back to the sacristy, going before him."],"varia-vi-11":["Of the servers of private Masses","11. There, having put down the Missal and the candle, prostrate at the priest&#x27;s feet he asks pardon if he has failed in anything in his service; then he helps the priest to take off the sacred vestments, and when he receives from him the stole, maniple, girdle or anything else, he kisses his hand (as was said). This done, he says to the priest <b class=\"rb-inc\">Benedictus Deus</b>; and the priest adding <b class=\"rb-inc\">Pater, et Filius, et Spiritus Sanctus</b>, the server answers <b class=\"rb-inc\">Amen</b>; and then he withdraws, having received the blessing."],"ministri-i-1":["Of the preparation of the ministers","1. When Mass is to be celebrated in the convent, the ministers of the altar are not to delay to vest when the signal is given. First the acolytes, vested in surplices, or in albs on Doubles and Totum Duplex feasts, help the other ministers. Nevertheless the deacon and subdeacon help each other, and both help the priest. In convents where the brethren are few, at the signal the deacon and subdeacon may vest without the silk vestments and so say the Office, if it is to be said, with the others in choir, until they leave the choir with the priest to assist him. In this vesting the capuces are to be so arranged under the church vestments that no noticeable hump ever appears on the brethren&#x27;s shoulders."],"ministri-i-2":["Of the preparation of the ministers","Then the acolytes prepare the altar, taking off the cover of the cloths and spreading the cloths becomingly; they bring one cushion for the Missal, to be placed at the Epistle corner towards the front, and another at the Gospel corner towards the back, and the book of Epistles, to be laid at the Epistle corner towards the back and propped there; and they see to the hosts and the cruets with wine and water, to be put in their place at the Epistle corner, and to a small cloth for wiping the nose, to be put beside the Missal; at the proper time, before Mass begins, they always light their own candles and those of the altar."],"ministri-i-3":["Of the preparation of the ministers","On Tuesday when the Mass is of St Dominic, on Wednesday when it is of St Thomas or another saint of our Order, on Thursday when it is of the most holy Body of Christ, and on Saturday when it is of the Blessed Virgin, and on Simple feasts, no more than two candles are lit; on Sundays and on Double and Totum Duplex feasts no more than four, except on the great solemnities, when at least six must be lit. The same is observed at Vespers and Matins. At the other Masses, even ferial or of the dead, not fewer than two are to be lit. In time of great cold the sacristan sees that lighted coals are kept in a fitting pan, to be brought by the acolytes at the proper time."],"ministri-ii-1":["Of the sprinkling of holy water","2. On Sundays the sacristan also provides holy water. When it is to be sprinkled, the acolytes going before, the subdeacon, deacon and priest, vested in albs with stoles and maniples only if there is to be no procession (otherwise the priest in a silk cope and the deacon and subdeacon in dalmatics), come to the sanctuary when the Hour before Mass is finished; when they arrive, the deacon stands with the senior acolyte at the priest&#x27;s right, the subdeacon with the junior at his left, and the priest in the middle; and all together bow profoundly, or genuflect, before the altar, as is said elsewhere."],"ministri-ii-2":["Of the sprinkling of holy water","Then the senior acolyte brings the priest the water, taken from the altar steps (where the sacristan has first placed it in a fitting vessel); and the deacon handing him the sprinkler, as the cantors begin the antiphon <b class=\"rb-inc\">Asperges me</b>, the priest first sprinkles the high altar itself, taking care not to throw too much water on it; then the deacon, subdeacon and acolytes; then the Master of the Order, if present; then the cantors, if it is a Double or Totum Duplex; then the brethren, beginning on the right, except when, there being no prelate in the right choir, some prior is in the left; or when, the prior of the convent being in the right choir, a greater prelate is in the left; for then the greater prelate is sprinkled first, then the prior, and then at once the right choir. If, however, the prelate in the left choir has some brother who is a Master holding a higher place, then the right choir is sprinkled first. So also when, some prelate who is not a Master being in the left choir, some Master is in the right."],"ministri-ii-3":["Of the sprinkling of holy water","After the clerical brethren the lay brothers are sprinkled, then only those seculars who are in choir, unless it is the custom that others outside the choir are sprinkled too. This order is kept in giving the peace, in incensing, and in the sprinkling of water after Compline, where it is the custom for it to be done by one only. If the choir is not next to the altar, another priest sprinkles the brethren in the same manner. When the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Asperges me</b> has been said by the community, the priest, standing before the sanctuary steps as at the beginning, says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Ostende nobis, etc.</b>, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b>, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Oremus</b>, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Exaudi nos, etc.</b> This said, the ministers return to the sacristy."],"ministri-ii-4":["Of the sprinkling of holy water","If there is to be a procession, while the water is sprinkled the crossbearer takes the cross prepared by the sacristan; and after the sprinkling the acolytes, returning to the sacristy, bring the candles and come with the crossbearer to the sanctuary, and go in procession with the community in the usual order; when it is over, the priest returns with the ministers to the sacristy, where he takes off the cope and puts on the chasuble."],"ministri-iii-1":["Of the entrance to the altar","3. So when the choir has begun the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria Patri</b> after the Introit, or the verse if the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria Patri</b> is not said, the ministers go to the altar with heads covered with capuce and amice: first the acolytes with fitting candlesticks and lighted candles; then the subdeacon with the book of the Gospels, or with the prepared chalice (when it is not to be brought after the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria in excelsis</b>, as will be said below), and the deacon with the Missal, each carrying his book before him resting against his breast, the left hand beneath and the right holding the book; last the priest with hands joined before his breast."],"ministri-iii-2":["Of the entrance to the altar","When they come before the altar and stand before it below the lowest step at some distance from one another, the deacon at the priest&#x27;s right, the subdeacon at his left, and the acolytes likewise one on each side, all together bow profoundly, or genuflect, and uncover their heads; and the priest, rising at once, having said secretly the prayer <b class=\"rb-inc\">Actiones nostras</b>, signing himself from forehead to breast and shoulders, says in an audible voice: <b class=\"rb-inc\">In nomine Patris, et Filii, etc.</b>, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Confitemini, etc.</b>; and while the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Confiteor Deo, etc.</b> is said they bow profoundly without bending the knees (which is always to be observed in such bows), the acolytes, however, standing upright, facing each other and holding the candlesticks in their hands."],"ministri-iii-3":["Of the entrance to the altar","The confession and absolution thus made, they stand erect, and the priest says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Adjutorium, etc.</b>, and going up to the altar, bowing not sideways but straight towards the middle of the altar (which is always to be observed in such bows), says the prayer <b class=\"rb-inc\">Aufer a nobis, etc.</b>, and standing erect again says secretly <b class=\"rb-inc\">In nomine Patris, etc.</b> Meanwhile the deacon puts the Missal at the Epistle corner, and the subdeacon the book of the Gospels at the Gospel corner, propping it at the back of the altar; and the acolytes set down the candles in their candlesticks on the sanctuary steps and put them out."],"ministri-iii-4":["Of the entrance to the altar","Then all come together at the book, already opened by the deacon (who must also turn the pages, and point out to the priest what is to be said, as often as is needed), and standing at the priest&#x27;s right in order of rank they say the Introit and the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Kyrie eleison</b> with the celebrant; nor do they kneel when <b class=\"rb-inc\">Salve sancta parens</b> is said."],"ministri-iii-5":["Of the entrance to the altar","4. Then the deacon places himself behind the priest, and the subdeacon behind the deacon. The acolytes stand below the sanctuary steps, one on each side (or in the first stalls of the choir, especially where the brethren are few), ready to perform their duties; yet so that while the Canon or anything else is said by the priest standing in the middle of the altar, if there are two acolytes, one stands at the right of the deacon and the other at the left, ranged in the form of a cross, while they are not busy with some duty. But throughout the whole celebration of Mass they never go up to the top step of the sanctuary, on which the priest stands."],"ministri-iii-6":["Of the entrance to the altar","If it is a Sunday or a Double or higher, all may go to sit, in this order: the priest sitting first in the sanctuary on the Epistle side (on which side such seats are always to be prepared), the deacon at his left, and the subdeacon at the deacon&#x27;s left; then the acolytes sit there too, or elsewhere, but on a lower bench (and this order is kept wherever the ministers of the altar sit). Before they go to sit, the acolytes spread a small cloth over the knees of the priest, deacon and subdeacon, so that they can also rest their hands on it; which one of the acolytes, rising at the last <b class=\"rb-inc\">Kyrie eleison</b>, afterwards takes up, beginning with the priest and kissing his hand."],"ministri-iv-1":["At the Gloria in excelsis Deo","5. This done, all rise and, standing before the middle of the altar, bow profoundly; and the priest, having gone up to the altar, begins the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria in excelsis</b> if it is to be said (if not, he turns to the people and says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b>), the other ministers being ranged behind him as was said. Then, coming together to the Epistle corner, they continue the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria</b> with the priest, standing at his right in the order given above. When it is said, the priest and deacon stand until the community has finished it. And the ministers are to take care, when something is to be done, not to pass between the ministers of higher rank, but behind them."],"ministri-iv-2":["At the Gloria in excelsis Deo","Meanwhile, while the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria in excelsis</b> is sung on Sundays and Double feasts, or the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Kyrie eleison</b> when the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria in excelsis</b> is not said from Septuagesima to Easter, the subdeacon, one of the acolytes going before, goes to the sacristy, and there spreads a fair, clean, prepared veil becomingly over his shoulders (the acolyte helping); he takes the chalice prepared as was said for private Mass; and so, the acolyte going before carrying the wine and water, he carries the chalice reverently, raised before his breast; and when they reach the altar they bow profoundly in the middle. Then the subdeacon sets the chalice on the altar, takes off the veil and spreads it becomingly on the Epistle corner, and sets the prepared chalice on it. The acolyte puts the wine and water in the niche, or on a bench at the Epistle corner."],"ministri-v-1":["At the Collects","6. When the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria in excelsis</b>, or the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Kyrie eleison</b> according to the season, has been said, as the priest begins <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b> the deacon, without kneeling unless a bishop is celebrating, lightly raises the chasuble by its lower edge, and so holding the chasuble goes with the priest to the book and points out to him the prayer to be said; which he will also do at the Offertory, the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Orate fratres</b> and the Postcommunion. <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b> having been said, the priest says the prayer."],"ministri-v-2":["At the Collects","If the Mass is of Lent, after <b class=\"rb-inc\">Oremus</b> the deacon, kneeling with the other lower ministers, says aloud <b class=\"rb-inc\">Flectamus genua</b>, and rising says at once <b class=\"rb-inc\">Levate</b>. The priest does not kneel, but goes on with the prayer; when it is finished he says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per omnia saecula saeculorum</b>, and the choir having answered <b class=\"rb-inc\">Amen</b>, the priest and deacon go to the middle of the altar and down to the lowest step, and all the ministers together with them bow profoundly before the altar and go to sit."],"ministri-vi-1":["At the Epistle","7. While the last prayer is said, one of the acolytes going before, the subdeacon carries the book of Epistles, resting against his breast in the manner described above for the Gospel book and the Missal, to the place where the Epistle is to be read; this will always be before the sanctuary steps, at a lectern prepared there in the middle, facing the altar. But in convents where it is the custom on Sundays and on Doubles and higher to read it in a raised place, from the pulpit, the custom is to be kept. The acolyte, when the Epistle has been read, carries the book handed him by the subdeacon to the altar and puts it in its place."],"ministri-vi-2":["At the Epistle","8. The deacon and the other acolyte, standing by, together reverently seat the priest in his seat; and the acolyte, or the deacon himself if there is no acolyte, lays a small cloth over his knees as above."],"ministri-vi-3":["At the Epistle","9. Then the deacon, having first washed his fingers, spreads the corporal in the middle of the altar, and afterwards, taking the Missal, hands it to the acolyte and comes to sit. The priest, then sitting, the acolyte (if there are two) or the deacon (if there is no acolyte) holding the Missal before him, says the Epistle, and then what is to be said of the Responsory or Tract and Sequence; and if the acolyte holds the Missal these are said verse by verse alternately by the priest and deacon, the priest beginning. These said, they rise, and the priest standing reads the Gospel with his joined hands laid upon the book, signing himself with the cross at the beginning and end, as was said above in the Rite of Mass."],"ministri-vi-4":["At the Epistle","This said, the subdeacon, when he has come back, carries the Missal to the Gospel corner for the singing of the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Credo</b> or the Offertory; then he washes his hands a little, spreads the veil over his shoulders with the acolyte&#x27;s help as said above, uncovers the chalice, and takes it with his left hand by the knot of the cup, steadying the pall and paten on top with his right; and coming before the eyes of the priest and of the deacon, who are seated, he prepares it in this way: he hands the paten with the host to the deacon, who will show it to the priest; then he takes the wine from the hand of the assisting acolyte and pours it into the chalice, but at one side, and in such quantity as the priest indicates; then he offers the water to the priest to be blessed, and when he has said <b class=\"rb-inc\">Benedicite</b> and the deacon has answered Dominus, the priest adds the blessing <b class=\"rb-inc\">In nomine Patris, et Filii, et Spiritus Sancti</b>, the acolyte answering <b class=\"rb-inc\">Amen</b>; and he lets a little water fall into the middle of the chalice, as was said; then he receives the paten with the host from the deacon, steadies it as above, carries it back to the middle of the altar, covers it as it was before, and then comes to sit."],"ministri-vii-1":["At the Tract","10. When the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Veni sancte Spiritus</b> is said in the Mass, at its beginning the priest and ministers rise, and do not kneel, but stand while Spiritus is sung, as far as reple; then, when the choir rises, the ministers sit. While the priest sits, the ministers, when they are not busy with some duty, may go to help the brethren in choir, especially in small convents."],"ministri-vii-2":["At the Tract","Towards the end of the chant of the Alleluia, Tract, Responsory or Sequence, according to the season, the acolytes light the candles; and the thurifer is present on Simple feasts and above, on Sundays, on Tuesday when it is of St Dominic, on Wednesday when it is of St Thomas or another saint of the Order, on Thursday when it is of the most holy Body of Christ, on Saturday when it is of the Blessed Virgin Mary, and on the octave day of simple octaves; he will stand in the sanctuary with the thurible before the middle of the altar."],"ministri-vii-3":["At the Tract","On Totum Duplex feasts, on which alone the cross is to be carried at the Gospel, after the Epistle has been sung, while the Responsory, Alleluia, Tract or Sequence is sung, as the case may be, the acolytes go to the sacristy carrying the candlesticks with lighted candles, the thurifer going before, and from there accompany the cross, the thurifer leading and the acolytes following with the crossbearer between them; and so they come before the altar steps, the crossbearer standing between the two acolytes and the thurifer at the right of the acolyte who is at the crossbearer&#x27;s right; and so all (except the crossbearer) bow profoundly. Then the thurifer goes to the priest and, offering him the incense, says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Benedicite</b>; and the priest, making the sign of the cross over the incense, says <b class=\"rb-inc\">In nomine Patris, et Filii, et Spiritus Sancti. Amen</b>."],"ministri-viii-1":["At the Gospel","11. Then the deacon rises and, taking the book of the Gospels in both hands, bowing a little before the priest, says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Jube domne benedicere</b>; and the priest, rising, answers <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus sit in corde tuo, et in labiis tuis, ad pronuntiandum sanctum Evangelium pacis</b>: <b class=\"rb-inc\">In nomine Patris, et Filii, etc.</b> <b class=\"rb-inc\">Amen</b>. And so they go to the pulpit: first the thurifer, second the candle-bearers with the crossbearer between them, third the subdeacon with the cushion prepared for the Gospel, last the deacon carrying the book of the Gospels resting against his breast in the manner described above."],"ministri-viii-2":["At the Gospel","The lectern will be prepared at the Gospel corner in a slightly raised place, facing north. But in convents where there is a pulpit behind the choir, or elsewhere in a higher place, the Gospel is read from it on Sundays and on Doubles and above. When they have reached the lectern, the subdeacon puts the cushion under the book; the crossbearer and the candle-bearers, one at his right and the other at his left, stand behind, before the lectern, with their own faces and the face of the Crucified turned towards the deacon; the subdeacon stands behind the deacon."],"ministri-viii-3":["At the Gospel","When the deacon says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Sequentia</b> or <b class=\"rb-inc\">Initium sancti Evangelii</b>, he makes a cross with his thumb on the beginning of the Gospel, and also one on his forehead, another on his mouth and another on his breast; then he signs his whole person with the sign of the cross from forehead to breast and shoulders (the priest and subdeacon do the same, facing the altar); then, taking the thurible from the subdeacon&#x27;s hand, he incenses the book of the Gospel three times, and with hands joined before his breast continues the Gospel. Meanwhile the thurifer places himself at the left of the acolyte who is at the crossbearer&#x27;s left, or behind the subdeacon, wherever it is convenient."],"ministri-viii-4":["At the Gospel","When the brethren say <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria tibi Domine</b>, they must turn to the altar and sign themselves with the cross, as said above. While the Gospel is read, the priest stands at the Epistle corner, facing the Gospel, with hands joined before his breast until it has been read; likewise all the brethren must turn their faces to the Gospel, wherever it is read; and when it is finished they must sign themselves with the cross from the head to the breast and shoulders."],"ministri-viii-5":["At the Gospel","12. When the Gospel is finished, the deacon hands the open book with the cushion to the subdeacon, and they return to the altar in the order in which they came. But if in some convents the pulpit is so placed that in going to it they pass through the choir, the thurifer passes through the middle of the choir and one candle-bearer along one side and the other along the other, while the crossbearer, the subdeacon and the deacon go along the right side, and on returning along the left."],"ministri-ix-1":["At the Credo","13. At the end of the Gospel the priest, coming to the middle of the altar, says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Credo in unum Deum</b>, if it is to be said. And when the ministers have reached the altar, the subdeacon offers the book to the priest to kiss, the deacon pointing out the Gospel to him with his finger (unless Mass is celebrated for the dead, or the Passion has been read); and the priest, signing the Gospel that was read with the cross with his thumb, says as he kisses it <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per Evangelica dicta, etc.</b>, and the deacon answers <b class=\"rb-inc\">Amen</b>. After him the deacon kisses it. After this the subdeacon puts the book back on the altar where and as it was before, to carry it back to the sacristy from there when convenient."],"ministri-ix-2":["At the Credo","Then the deacon and all the other ministers, standing at the priest&#x27;s left, ranged one behind another towards the Gospel corner, continue the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Credo in unum Deum</b> in a low voice; and when the choir has come to the words <b class=\"rb-inc\">Et incarnatus est</b>, the priest kneels before the middle of the altar; likewise the deacon and subdeacon kneel on either side on the first step of the altar behind the priest; and behind them, on another, lower step, the acolytes kneel ranged on either side, so that the crossbearer is between them and the thurifer behind the crossbearer, all facing the altar."],"ministri-ix-3":["At the Credo","Afterwards all rise, and the acolytes and thurifer, having first bowed before the altar, accompany the cross to the sacristy in the order in which they came, the thurifer going before, who must always be behind the subdeacon except when the Ordinarium directs otherwise. And when the words <b class=\"rb-inc\">simul adoratur, etc.</b> are said in the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Credo</b>, all must bow."],"ministri-x-1":["At the Offertory","14. When the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Credo</b> is finished, or the Gospel if the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Credo</b> is not said, the priest says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b>, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Oremus</b>, and the Offertory, which the ministers continue with him, all standing ranged at his left as above; this said, returning to the middle of the altar he says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Quid retribuam Domino pro omnibus, quae retribuit mihi?</b> Meanwhile the subdeacon uncovers the chalice of veil and pall and offers it to the deacon; and the deacon, holding it by the lower part of the foot, offers it to the priest, kissing his hand (unless the Most Holy Sacrament is exposed), and says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Immola Deo sacrificium laudis, et redde Altissimo vota tua</b>; and the priest, taking it, says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Calicem salutaris accipiam, et nomen Domini invocabo</b>, and then, holding it a little raised in both hands, says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Suscipe, etc.</b>"],"ministri-xi-1":["Of the incensing of the altar","15. Then, the thurifer holding the thurible at the right side of the altar, and the deacon offering the priest in the spoon incense taken from the boat in which it is kept, for the blessing, the priest blesses it in the manner written above; this done, the deacon puts it into the thurible and, taking the thurible, hands it at once to the priest, kissing his hand (if the Most Holy Sacrament is not exposed). The priest, taking it in his right hand, hanging a short way down the chains, and holding the ends of the chains in his left, makes the sign of the cross with the thurible over the chalice, then bows his head, and then incenses three times before it, not swinging the thurible high, but with his hand lowered a little with the thurible and then raised a little, so that the hands are never lifted above the shoulder."],"ministri-xi-2":["Of the incensing of the altar","Then he incenses three times the tabernacle of the most holy Body of Christ, if it is on the altar, or the cross if it is not; then, bowing profoundly with the deacon at the middle of the altar (which is always to be done in passing from one side of the altar to the other), he incenses the top of the altar towards the candlesticks, swinging the thurible three times at equal distances as far as the Epistle corner; from there, with his hand lowered, he returns to the middle of the altar, and going on from there incenses the other side in the same way as far as the Gospel corner; then he incenses the front of the altar, likewise with three swings, going from the Gospel corner to the middle, and afterwards, also with three swings, the rest of the front as far as the Epistle corner. While the priest incenses, the deacon becomingly raises his chasuble, so that he can incense freely."],"ministri-xi-3":["Of the incensing of the altar","16. If there are relics or images of the saints on the altar, when the tabernacle, or the cross if there is none, has been incensed and reverence made to it as above, before he leaves the middle of the altar he first incenses those on the right, that is on the Gospel side near the cross, swinging the thurible twice; and, having meanwhile made reverence to the tabernacle or cross, he likewise incenses the others, on the left, that is on the Epistle side; this done, he continues the incensing of the altar as above, swinging the thurible three times on each side, however many relics or images there may be on it, or more or fewer candlesticks (the same is observed at Vespers and Matins when the incensing is to be done)."],"ministri-xi-4":["Of the incensing of the altar","Meanwhile, while the incensing is done at Mass, the other ministers range themselves at the Epistle corner, so that the subdeacon is near the altar, and at his left the acolytes and the thurifer in their order, one below the other. When the priest has finished incensing he holds his hands joined before his breast, standing at the end of the altar where he finished incensing, facing the deacon. The deacon, taking the thurible from the priest&#x27;s hand, incenses him with three swings, lightly raising the chasuble with his left hand and holding the thurible in his right; then he gives it back to the thurifer."],"ministri-xii-1":["At the washing of the hands, and at the Secrets","17. When the deacon has incensed the priest, the priest washes his hands, the subdeacon pouring the water. While the subdeacon pours the water, the deacon, taking the cloth or towel from the acolyte, hands it to the priest to dry his hands; and while the priest washes his hands he says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Lavabo, etc.</b>, and afterwards <b class=\"rb-inc\">In spiritu, etc.</b>"],"ministri-xii-2":["At the washing of the hands, and at the Secrets","18. Then he says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Orate fratres</b>, etc., only these two words in a moderate voice, so that they can be heard by the ministers; afterwards he says the secret prayers, the deacon raising the chasuble as said above. In the season of flies, too, the deacon must keep them off with a fan, lest they trouble the priest, and drive them from the sacrifice."],"ministri-xiii-1":["Of the incensing of the ministers and the community","19. While the Preface is said the thurifer prepares to incense, and when the words <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gratias agamus Domino Deo nostro</b> have been sung, having first bowed before the altar, he incenses the ministers of the altar: first the deacon, then the subdeacon and the acolytes, who place themselves to be incensed so that the deacon with one acolyte at the Gospel corner and the subdeacon with the other at the Epistle corner almost face each other, with hands joined before the breast and bowing the head a little while they are incensed."],"ministri-xiii-2":["Of the incensing of the ministers and the community","These incensed, the thurifer goes into the choir and incenses the Master of the Order, if present; then the others (according to what was said of the holy water), but with unblessed incense, which he puts afresh into the thurible; and he begins with the seniors in the upper stalls, and turning round to the juniors in the lower stalls, if there are brethren there, continues the incensing in both choirs, and then the lay brothers. This done, he returns to the altar and stands in the middle behind the subdeacon."],"ministri-xiv-1":["At the Sanctus, and at the Canon","20. Then, the Preface having been said, when <b class=\"rb-inc\">supplici confessione, etc.</b> is reached, the deacon with one of the acolytes (who warns those present with a few strokes of the bell) comes to the Gospel corner, and the subdeacon with the other to the Epistle corner, and facing each other they say the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Sanctus, etc.</b> with the priest, and at <b class=\"rb-inc\">Benedictus qui venit</b> sign themselves with the cross from head to breast and shoulders. The brethren in choir are also to do this when they say Benedictus, and they are not to turn to the altar then."],"ministri-xiv-2":["At the Sanctus, and at the Canon","21. Then, if it is a Sunday, or a Simple feast or greater, or a solemn octave, or the octave day of simple octaves, or a Tuesday on which St Dominic is kept, or a Thursday of the Most Holy Sacrament, or a Saturday of the Blessed Virgin, or another day on which a saint of the Order is celebrated, the subdeacon takes the veil and spreads it over his shoulders, and the deacon hands him the paten and covers it with the chalice veil, or folds the veil back over it; and the acolytes light the candles for the elevation (on other days the paten is not held by the subdeacon). Then the subdeacon, standing behind the deacon, holds the paten raised in his hand until after the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Pater noster</b>, putting his left hand under his right arm."],"ministri-xv-1":["At the consecration","22. Meanwhile, while the priest pronounces the words of consecration, the deacon at the priest&#x27;s right and the subdeacon with the paten at his left kneel on the top step of the altar, one acolyte standing on a lower step beside the deacon and the other beside the subdeacon, and one rings the bell; and so they remain from the time of the elevation of the Host until after the consecration and elevation of the Blood. On the days on which incense is used, the deacon takes the thurible from the thurifer, or from one of the acolytes where the brethren are few, and incenses continuously during the elevation of the Body and of the Blood; afterwards he gives the thurible back to the thurifer or acolyte, who carries it back to the sacristy and leaves it there."],"ministri-xv-2":["At the consecration","23. At all times the choir must so avoid excessive length of chant in singing the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Sanctus, etc.</b>, and the priest must so deliberately complete what is said before the elevation of the Host, that the elevation never takes place before that chant has ended."],"ministri-xvi-1":["At the Pater noster","24. When the priest begins the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Pater noster</b>, on the days noted above the subdeacon uncovers with his left hand the paten which he holds in his right; and while the priest says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Et ne nos inducas in tentationem</b>, the deacon with the other ministers answers in a low voice <b class=\"rb-inc\">Sed libera nos a malo</b>, and the priest <b class=\"rb-inc\">Amen</b>; and then the subdeacon hands the paten to the deacon, who receives it with his bare hand and gives it to the priest when he is about to say <b class=\"rb-inc\">Da propitius pacem</b>; the priest receives it in his right hand between the forefinger and middle finger, the thumb and forefinger not being parted; and the deacon, as he gives it, kisses his shoulder (unless the most holy Body of Christ is exposed on the altar); and the priest, signing himself with the paten as at private Mass and then kissing it, lays it on the altar apart from the corporal."],"ministri-xvi-2":["At the Pater noster","And while the priest signs himself with the paten, the acolytes reverently fold the veil, and the one on the left puts it on the Gospel corner. When the subdeacon has not held the paten, the deacon nevertheless takes it from the altar after the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Pater noster</b>, as was said, and offers it to the priest in the manner described."],"ministri-xvii-1":["At the Agnus Dei and the peace","25. When the priest has said <b class=\"rb-inc\">Pax Domini sit semper vobiscum</b>, the deacon with one acolyte goes to the Gospel corner and the subdeacon with the other to the Epistle corner, and facing each other they say the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Agnus Dei, etc.</b> with him. Then, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Haec sacrosancta commixtio, etc.</b> having been said, the priest kisses the lip of the chalice; afterwards he kisses the paten, or some holy image (which is commonly called the Pax), held out to him at once by the deacon, saying in a low voice <b class=\"rb-inc\">Pax tibi, et Ecclesiae sanctae Dei</b> (which he does not do in the Triduum before Easter, nor in Masses for the dead). Then the deacon gives it to the subdeacon to kiss, and the subdeacon to the acolytes, who for this reason must then be in the middle behind the subdeacon."],"ministri-xvii-2":["At the Agnus Dei and the peace","On Sundays and on Doubles and above, after the acolytes have received the peace from the subdeacon as was said, one of them gives it to the cantors (unless the Master of the Order is present); and the cantor gives it to the brethren in the order described above for the holy water, after the whole chant of the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Agnus Dei</b> has ended. On other days the peace is never given at the conventual Mass except to the ministers of the Mass."],"ministri-xvii-3":["At the Agnus Dei and the peace","Further, on the great solemnities, namely Christmas, Easter, the Ascension, Pentecost, the most holy Body of Christ, the Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary, our Blessed Father Dominic, All Saints and the like, the subdeacon, after giving the peace to the acolytes as stated, carries it to the cantors in the choir, one of the acolytes going before, and then to the rest of the brethren in the order and manner described. But where the choir is not next to the altar, the subdeacon does not carry the peace unless he can conveniently return before the end of the Communion; it is carried instead by the thurifer or by one of the acolytes."],"ministri-xviii-1":["At the Communion","26. So the priest, having given the peace to the deacon, says the prayer <b class=\"rb-inc\">Domine Jesu Christe, etc.</b>, and <b class=\"rb-inc\">Corpus et Sanguis Domini nostri, etc.</b>, inserting no other prayers and not striking his breast; and when he has communicated he comes to the Epistle corner, and there the subdeacon, having wine ready in a cruet, pours it into the chalice, and afterwards wine and water. Meanwhile the deacon carries the Missal to the Epistle corner and, having first washed his fingers, folds the corporal and puts it back in the burse. The priest, having taken the second ablution, sets down the chalice, and the subdeacon, having also first washed his fingers, takes it and purifies it, and puts on it the purificator, paten, pall, veil, the burse of the corporal and the small veil; and the acolytes put out the candles lit at the elevation."],"ministri-xix-1":["At the Postcommunion","27. Then the priest says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b>, and the deacon raises the chasuble as before."],"ministri-xix-2":["At the Postcommunion","28. When the Mass is of a Lenten feria, after all the commemorations, the priest having said <b class=\"rb-inc\">Oremus</b>, bowing his head with the other ministers, the deacon adds aloud <b class=\"rb-inc\">Humiliate capita vestra Deo</b>, and the priest continues with the prayer marked &quot;over the people&quot;."],"ministri-xix-3":["At the Postcommunion","29. While the last prayers are said, the subdeacon, one of the acolytes going before carrying the wine and water, carries the chalice, arranged as was said, back to the sacristy, without the veil spread over his shoulders. When he has come back to the altar, one of the acolytes hands him the book of the Gospel, and the other lights the candles on the candlesticks. But if only one prayer is to be said, or the sacristy is too far from the altar at which Mass is celebrated, the subdeacon does not carry the chalice back to the sacristy until Mass is over, when he returns to the sacristy with the priest and the others; and afterwards one of the acolytes carries the book of the Gospels."],"ministri-xx-1":["At the Ite Missa est, and after the blessing","30. When the priest turns to the community to say <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b>, the deacon turns likewise and says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Ite Missa est</b>, if the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria in excelsis</b> has gone before; otherwise the deacon does not turn to the community, but facing the altar, the priest also facing it, says <b class=\"rb-inc\">Benedicamus Domino</b>, or <b class=\"rb-inc\">Requiescant in pace</b> if the Mass is of the dead. The priest and deacon stand turned to the community only while the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Ite Missa est</b> is said; then they turn back to the altar without going round."],"ministri-xx-2":["At the Ite Missa est, and after the blessing","31. Meanwhile, after the priest has given the blessing, at which the ministers must bow their heads, the senior acolyte hands the Missal to the deacon and the other the book of the Gospels to the subdeacon (if he is not to carry the chalice), and then they hold the candlesticks with lighted candles in their hands while the Gospel is read; and the deacon and subdeacon go up to the priest&#x27;s left at the Gospel corner to hear it. When the Gospel has been said, and a bow or genuflexion made as at the entrance, all return to the sacristy in the order in which they came. Then the ministers first unvest the priest, and afterwards help one another to unvest."],"ministri-xx-3":["At the Ite Missa est, and after the blessing","32. The acolytes, before they take off their vestments, go back to the altar, cover it and put it in good order, carrying back to the sacristy what is to be carried back. Afterwards, on Sundays, they carry holy water through the cells and the other offices, sprinkling them."],"ministri-xx-4":["At the Ite Missa est, and after the blessing","33. Where on solemn days the solemnity of the ministers described cannot be kept because the brethren are few, it is to be observed as conveniently and becomingly as possible. On ferial days, on feasts of three lessons, and within non-solemn octaves, one acolyte suffices; he must stand at the right beside the deacon during the confession, while the Sanctus and the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Agnus Dei</b> are said by the priest, and also at the genuflexion at the Body of the Lord."],"communio-p-1":["Of Holy Communion (the Communion of the brethren in the convent)","1. Concerning Holy Communion and the manner of communicating in the convent, it is to be noted that, for reserving Holy Communion, there must be a fitting and seemly tabernacle on the altar, or in another fitting place, in which the Most Holy Sacrament of the Body of Christ is honourably placed. Inside it there is to be a pyx of silver, or of other precious material, in which the consecrated hosts are kept, yet not in great number. Care is to be taken that both the tabernacle and the pyx are so securely fixed and closed that the hosts cannot by any chance fall or be removed, except by the ministry of the brethren when some Communion is to be given."],"communio-p-2":["Of Holy Communion (the Communion of the brethren in the convent)","2. On a Communion day it is customary that some exhortation be given in Chapter, or a general absolution to the brethren, clerics and lay brothers alike, so that they may approach so great a Sacrament more worthily prepared. On such a day the prelate, if he conveniently can, or one of the senior Fathers, should celebrate the conventual Mass."],"communio-p-3":["Of Holy Communion (the Communion of the brethren in the convent)","3. When the subdeacon is to supply what is needed for this Sacrament, he must ask the sacristan, whose business it is to know, the number of consecrated hosts in the pyx; he must also ask the number of brethren who are to communicate; and having compared them, he puts out as many hosts to be consecrated as will suffice for the brethren communicating and for the sick in case of need."],"communio-p-4":["Of Holy Communion (the Communion of the brethren in the convent)","4. The celebrating priest who is to consecrate several hosts puts them in a consecrated chalice, or in a consecrated pyx near the chalice, but always on the corporal; if there is no separate vessel, he places them on the corporal before the chalice, if because of their number they cannot stay on the paten. At the consecration he pronounces the words of the form over the host which he holds in his hands, and over all the others which he will have exposed before his eyes. After the consecration he arranges them in a fitting place on the corporal, so that he can continue the other ceremonies without hindrance."],"communio-p-5":["Of Holy Communion (the Communion of the brethren in the convent)","5. So when the hour of Communion draws near, the brethren who are to communicate come towards the sanctuary, ranged as in choir, and there, having bowed profoundly, they kneel. The deacon, if there is no tabernacle containing consecrated hosts in a pyx on the altar where Mass is celebrated, brings the pyx with the most holy Body of the Lord, the candle-bearers going before with candles, and sets it on the corporal, opening it at once; and all the brethren, except the celebrating priest, who alone adores genuflecting, on seeing him carry the pyx prostrate themselves on their knees, adoring the Most Holy Sacrament, until he has laid it on the corporal."],"communio-p-6":["Of Holy Communion (the Communion of the brethren in the convent)","This done, kneeling upright and having made a short prayer, the ministers again prostrate as before, and the others stretched at full length in the manner of the venia, all say the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Confiteor</b>, etc. together; and the priest, stepping a little aside from the middle of the altar so as not to turn his back to the Most Holy Sacrament, gives the absolution, saying <b class=\"rb-inc\">Misereatur, etc.</b> and <b class=\"rb-inc\">Absolutionem, etc.</b>"],"communio-p-7":["Of Holy Communion (the Communion of the brethren in the convent)","6. Afterwards all raise themselves on their knees, and first the ministers communicate according to the order of their rank: coming before the middle of the altar, they bow profoundly together below the lowest step, and going up to the top step bow profoundly there again, and at once kneel, keeping the rest of the body upright, avoiding any rolling of the eyes, unusual disarrangement of the face or undisciplined movement, and with the mouth moderately open they receive with due reverence the most holy Body of Christ from the priest&#x27;s hand. Having received Communion, they rise together, bow profoundly together, and withdraw facing one another, stepping sideways so as not to turn their backs to the Most Holy Sacrament; and below the lowest step of the altar they again bow profoundly before the Most Holy, and then kneel in the sanctuary."],"communio-p-8":["Of Holy Communion (the Communion of the brethren in the convent)","7. Meanwhile, while the ministers communicate, the other brethren, wearing the cappa of the Order, come two by two in their order one after another before the middle of the altar, and when they arrive there bow profoundly below the steps likewise; and as the ministers withdraw from the top step they take their place, and having also bowed profoundly they kneel; and having received Communion they withdraw in the same order and manner as described for the ministers, except that they do not remain kneeling in the sanctuary, but return to their places in choir and pray apart in silence in thanksgiving. And all are to take care not to spit soon after Communion; if they cannot avoid it, they must spit in a seemly place."],"communio-p-9":["Of Holy Communion (the Communion of the brethren in the convent)","8. First, when the ministers are to communicate, the deacon and subdeacon, kneeling on the upper step of the altar on either side and facing each other, hold a clean and seemly cloth prepared for this, stretched between the priest and those communicating, and those communicating raise it under their chin with both hands; after the acolytes have communicated, they hold this cloth in the same way until all have communicated."],"communio-p-10":["Of Holy Communion (the Communion of the brethren in the convent)","9. The priest first gives the consecrated hosts that were reserved in the pyx, and afterwards those newly consecrated, so that few remain after Communion to be put back and kept in the pyx. As he gives each host, he takes it with the thumb and forefinger of the right hand, and the pyx or paten with the left, holding it beneath the host, and so carries it to the brother who is to communicate. And as he gives it, making the sign of the cross with the host before the mouth of the communicant, he says, putting nothing else before it: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Corpus Domini nostri Jesu Christi custodiat te in vitam aeternam. Amen</b>."],"communio-p-11":["Of Holy Communion (the Communion of the brethren in the convent)","Nor does he give them a blessing when the Communion is over, but, setting the pyx on the corporal, he continues the Mass, since he will give the blessing at the end. But if the priest gives Communion after Mass, or outside Mass, then when it is over, having put the pyx in the tabernacle, he gives the blessing, saying: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Benedictio Dei omnipotentis, Patris, et Filii, et Spiritus Sancti descendat super vos, et maneat semper. Amen</b>."],"communio-p-12":["Of Holy Communion (the Communion of the brethren in the convent)","10. If some people from outside also wish to communicate with us, he may give them Communion if there are enough hosts. If there are not, they may be given Communion at some lesser altar. It is to be noted, however, that if a great crowd is present waiting for the end of Mass, or because of some other hindrance, the Communion may be put off until after Mass (except on Maundy Thursday) if the prior sees fit; otherwise it is to be given before the Communion of the Mass is said. Those in choir who do not communicate lie prostrate on the forms meanwhile."],"communio-p-13":["Of Holy Communion (the Communion of the brethren in the convent)","11. When Communion is over, if the priest sees that some consecrated hosts remain, even if they are many, he must put them all back in the pyx, and so placed they are put away by the deacon in the tabernacle, if it is on the altar; otherwise they are carried honourably to the proper place, the candle-bearers going before and the brethren prostrate as described above."],"communio-p-14":["Of Holy Communion (the Communion of the brethren in the convent)","12. Where it is the custom to take wine after Communion, when the host has been consumed they come to take it; it is to be received standing, and in a small quantity, to rinse the mouth carefully, lest any particles of the host remain between the teeth."],"communio-p-15":["Of Holy Communion (the Communion of the brethren in the convent)","13. The deacon must receive this wine in a chalice other than the chalice with which Mass is celebrated, and then minister it to the others, standing at the Gospel corner. He must hold the foot of the chalice with a clean and seemly cloth, with which the brethren also wipe their lips after rinsing the mouth."],"communio-p-16":["Of Holy Communion (the Communion of the brethren in the convent)","14. If, however, there is a great number communicating (as on Maundy Thursday), the subdeacon, standing at the Epistle corner, may minister wine in another chalice in the same way, so that all may be finished more quickly; or the brethren may also take for themselves wine prepared separately by the sacristan behind the altar."],"communio-p-17":["Of Holy Communion (the Communion of the brethren in the convent)","15. The brethren must communicate on every Sunday of Advent and of Lent; on Christmas Day, the Circumcision, the Epiphany, Maundy Thursday, Easter, the Ascension, Pentecost, Trinity Sunday and the most holy Body of Christ; also on all feasts of Blessed Mary ever Virgin, and on the feast of All Saints; also on the feast of the Apostles Peter and Paul, on the feasts of our holy Father Dominic and of the saints of the Order, and on all the Sundays appointed for the confraternities of the most holy Rosary and of the Holy Name of God, because of the plenary indulgence granted on those days. Otherwise they will communicate twice a month, that is every fortnight, if the prelate sees fit. No one is to stay away from this Communion on the said days without special leave; those who are outside the convent must communicate outside, or when they return, as soon as they conveniently can."],"communio-p-18":["Of Holy Communion (the Communion of the brethren in the convent)","16. When several Communion days follow one another immediately, the brethren may be dispensed from Communion on some of them at the prelate&#x27;s discretion."],"tit-i-1":["Of Feasts","1. Feasts Totum Duplex, Duplex, Semiduplex, Simplex and of three Lessons are always celebrated on their own day, unless they are impeded by a higher Office or feast, as is set out below in title VII."],"tit-i-2":["Of Feasts","2. On feasts Totum Duplex and Duplex only one Prayer is said, unless some Commemoration occurs which must be made; this, however, is sometimes to be left out, according to title VIII."],"tit-i-3":["Of Feasts","3. On feasts Semiduplex and below, outside a solemn or greater Octave, at least three Prayers are said, as set out below in title X."],"tit-ii-1":["Of Octaves","1. Within Octaves, when their Office is said, the Mass is said as on the feast itself, unless a proper Mass is given for each day of the Octave. The <b class=\"rb-inc\">Credo</b>, however, is said daily only within the most solemn Octaves."],"tit-ii-2":["Of Octaves","2. If the Office of the Octave is impeded by a feast or a Sunday, the Commemoration of the Octave is made or omitted in the Mass of the feast or Sunday, as the Rubrics of the Breviary say for each case."],"tit-ii-3":["Of Octaves","3. When the Office is of a solemn Octave or above, only one Prayer is said, unless a Commemoration must be made. Through a simple Octave and on an Octave day of three Lessons, several Prayers are said, as set out in title X."],"tit-iii-1":["Of the Sunday","1. Throughout the year the Mass of the Sunday is always to be celebrated, unless a feast which is to be kept on Sundays impedes it, and except when the Vigils of Christmas and of the Epiphany fall on a Sunday."],"tit-iii-2":["Of the Sunday","2. Three Prayers are said, as prescribed below in title X, except on Palm Sunday and Low Sunday, when only one is said, and except in the cases given in title VIII."],"tit-iii-3":["Of the Sunday","3. When, because of these impediments, the Sunday Mass cannot be celebrated on its own day, it is always to be said (unless otherwise noted in its proper place) within its own week on a free Feria on which the Homily of the Sunday is read; it is then celebrated according to the rite of the Office said that day, with the fitting Commemoration or Commemorations."],"tit-iii-4":["Of the Sunday","4. On such a day of resumption, if a feast Totum Duplex, Duplex, Semiduplex or Simplex occurs, two conventual Masses are said, one in Choir and the other outside Choir, as will be said below in title IV, n. 2; but on feasts and Octave days of three Lessons, within Octaves, and on Ferias, only one conventual Mass, of the Sunday itself. Private Masses may be said either of the Office of the day with a Commemoration of the Sunday, or of the Sunday with a Commemoration of the Office of the day, adding in either Mass any other Commemorations that occur. The same is done (even though the Homily is omitted) with the Mass of a Sunday which cannot be resumed after the Octave of the Epiphany or after Trinity; its Gospel, however, is read at the end of all the Masses in which that Sunday has been commemorated. — If the Vigil of Christmas falls on the fourth Sunday of Advent, the Mass of the Vigil is said in Choir, and outside Choir the Mass of the Sunday with its own rite. Likewise the Mass of the fourth Sunday after the Octave of Easter, when impeded, is said on the Sunday itself outside Choir. — Similarly the Mass of a Sunday whose Homily cannot be read during the week is said on the Sunday itself outside Choir. — If on the same weekday the Mass of a Sunday and that of a Vigil occur, the Mass of the Sunday is said in Choir with a Commemoration of the Vigil."],"tit-iv-1":["Of the Feria and the Vigil","1. The Mass of the Feria is celebrated when no feast or Octave, simple or above, occurs, and no Mass for the dead is said. On Ferias which have no Mass of their own, the Mass of the preceding Sunday is said; or the votive Masses may be said, according to the order of the Ferias, of the Saint or Office privileged in our Order, or, for private Masses, other votive Masses as well, which are treated in title V below."],"tit-iv-2":["Of the Feria and the Vigil","2. On all the Ferias of Lent after Ash Wednesday and of Passiontide before Palm Sunday, on Ember days outside the Octave of Pentecost, and on common Vigils that have a Mass of their own, if an Office Totum Duplex, Duplex, Semiduplex or Simplex occurs, two conventual Masses are said in churches bound to Choir: one of the Office of the day, without a Commemoration of the Feria or Vigil but with any other Commemorations that occur; the other of the Feria or Vigil, without a Commemoration of the Office of the day. Only one of them, however, is said in Choir with the choir assisting; the other is read outside Choir without the choir. The Mass said in Choir with the choir assisting is, on feasts Totum Duplex of the I and II class and through the privileged Octaves of the Epiphany and of Corpus Christi, the Mass of the current Office; on the other feasts Totum Duplex, Duplex, Semiduplex and Simplex, the Mass of the Feria or Vigil. On feasts Totum Duplex of the I class, however, nothing at all is done of a non-privileged Vigil (except the Vigil of the Ascension, which is a greater feria), according to the Rubrics. Of a day within a non-privileged Octave falling on the Ferias or Vigils listed above, no conventual Mass is said; it is only commemorated in the Mass of the Feria or Vigil. Private Masses on all these days may be said, as the celebrant chooses, either of the Feria or Vigil with a Commemoration of the Office of the day, or of the Office of the day with a Commemoration of the Feria or Vigil and the Gospel of the Feria at the end. If, however, the occurring Office is Totum Duplex of the I or II class, or the privileged Octave of the Epiphany or of Corpus Christi, a private Mass of the Feria or Vigil is not allowed."],"tit-iv-3":["Of the Feria and the Vigil","3. If one of these Ferias and a Vigil occur together, the conventual Mass is of the Feria with a Commemoration of the Vigil, and private Masses may be either of the Feria or of the Vigil with a Commemoration of the other. Or, if the Office is of a feast Totum Duplex, Duplex, a privileged Octave, Semiduplex or Simplex, two conventual Masses are said as above: one of the Office of the day, without a Commemoration of the Feria and Vigil but with any Commemorations that occur; the other of the Feria with a Commemoration of the Vigil. Private Masses may then be of the Feria, of the Vigil, or of the Office of the day, with a Commemoration of those whose Mass is not said and the Gospel of the Feria at the end."],"tit-iv-4":["Of the Feria and the Vigil","4. Likewise on 25 April (or, if 25 April is Easter Day, on the Tuesday after Easter) and on the Rogation days, if an Office Totum Duplex, Duplex, of a privileged Octave, Semiduplex or Simplex occurs (whether or not the Procession is held), two conventual Masses are said, as said above in n. 2: one of the Rogations (or of the Vigil of the Ascension with a Commemoration of the Rogations), the other of the Office of the day. On feasts Totum Duplex of the I and II class, however, or within the Octave of Easter, if the Procession is held, both the Mass of the Office of the day and the Mass of the Rogations are said in Choir; if it is not held, a single conventual Mass is said, of the day, with a Commemoration of the Feria (or Vigil) and the Gospel of the Rogations at the end. On all the days of the Litanies, unless the Office was of the Feria, private Masses of the Rogations are not allowed; instead the Commemoration of the Feria (or Vigil) and the last Gospel of the Rogations are said in all private Masses that are not Masses for the Dead."],"tit-iv-5":["Of the Feria and the Vigil","5. Where the brethren are few, only one conventual Mass is celebrated on these Ferias or Vigils when they fall with a feast, as is said in n. 6 following."],"tit-iv-6":["Of the Feria and the Vigil","6. In churches that have only one conventual Mass, whenever one of the Ferias or Vigils listed in nn. 2 and 3 occurs, and when the Procession is not held on the Rogation days, that conventual Mass is said, on feasts Totum Duplex of the I and II class and through the privileged Octaves of the Epiphany and of Corpus Christi, of the Office of the day with a Commemoration of the Feria and its Gospel at the end, and, outside feasts Totum Duplex of the I class, with a Commemoration of the Vigil as well. On the other feasts Totum Duplex, and on Duplex, Semiduplex and Simplex feasts, the same Mass is said of the Feria or Vigil with a Commemoration of the Office of the day; and if a Feria and a Vigil occur together, of the Feria, with Commemorations of the Office of the day, of the Vigil and of any others that occur (or, on the Vigil of the Ascension, of the Vigil itself with a Commemoration of the Rogations). Likewise on the days of the Litanies, if the Procession is held and the church has only one Mass, it is said of the Rogations (or of the Vigil of the Ascension) with a Commemoration of the Office of the day; unless that is a feast Totum Duplex of the I class, in which case the Mass of the feast is said with a Commemoration of the Feria (or Vigil) and the Gospel of the Rogations at the end."],"tit-v-1":["Of Votive Masses","1. On lesser Ferias, when neither the Mass of a Sunday is to be resumed nor a Mass for the dead to be celebrated, instead of the Mass of the preceding Sunday a votive conventual Mass may be said, according to the order of the Ferias, of the Saint or Office formerly privileged in our Order: on Tuesday of our holy Father Dominic, on Wednesday of St Thomas Aquinas, and on Thursday of the Blessed Sacrament or of our Lord Jesus Christ the High and Eternal Priest. These votive Masses, except that of our Lord Jesus Christ the Priest, are said outside Septuagesima with the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria</b> and three Alleluias after the Epistle or Responsory, and within that time with the Tract. On Tuesday the Sequence is also said. On Friday the Mass of the Holy Cross may be said, or for the brethren who have died that year, or of St Vincent (once a month), or another, according to the custom of the Provinces, but with the ferial rite. On Saturday the votive Mass of Our Lady proper to the season is said, if her votive Office has been said."],"tit-v-2":["Of Votive Masses","2. Private votive Masses without chant are forbidden on all Offices Totum Duplex and Duplex; on Sundays, even when resumed or transferred as to the Mass; through privileged Octaves; on Ember days; on the Ferias of Advent from 17 to 23 December inclusive; on the Ferias of Lent from Ash Wednesday, and of Passiontide, to Wednesday of Holy Week inclusive; on the Rogation days; on Vigils; and on Octave days, solemn, simple and of three Lessons, even if they have only been commemorated in the Office. They may therefore be read only, and for a reasonable cause, on Semiduplex and Simplex feasts, on days within non-privileged Octaves, on the Friday after the Octave of the Ascension, on feasts of three Lessons, and on the Ferias not excepted above."],"tit-v-3":["Of Votive Masses","3. The votive Mass for Bride and Bridegroom, with its own blessing, is allowed every day of the year outside the closed time, and in the closed time too whenever the local Ordinary has allowed the nuptial blessing for a just cause. Excepted are Sundays, feasts of obligation (even if suppressed), feasts Totum Duplex of the I and II class, privileged Octaves (but not those of Christmas, the Ascension and the Sacred Heart), and privileged Ferias and Vigils. In these excepted cases the Mass of the day is said, the Prayer for the Bride and Bridegroom is added under one conclusion with the first, and their own blessing is given. On the Commemoration of All the Faithful Departed, however, the Mass, the Prayer and the blessing are all forbidden. This blessing can never be given outside Mass; and where neither the Mass for the Bride and Bridegroom nor its Commemoration is possible, the blessing cannot take place either; nor if the couple are not present, or if both or either have received the blessing before (keeping, however, wherever it exists, the custom of giving it as above if only the husband has received it). If the marriage was contracted in the closed time without the local Ordinary&#x27;s leave for the nuptial blessing, the Mass with its own blessing is deferred to a day outside the closed time on which the Mass for Bride and Bridegroom, or its Commemoration, is allowed."],"tit-v-4":["Of Votive Masses","4. A solemn votive Mass for a grave and at the same time public cause, sung with the people assembled by order or with the consent of the local Ordinary, obtained each time, is allowed on every day except Sundays of the I class, the Vigils of Christmas and Pentecost, privileged Ferias, feasts Totum Duplex of the I class and the Commemoration of All the Faithful Departed. This Mass cannot be sung, however, on a day which has an Office or a Commemoration, or a Vigil or a day within an Octave (even one only commemorated), of the same Mystery of our Lord or the same Saint as the Mass to be sung; for then, instead of the votive Mass, the Mass proper to that Office, Commemoration, Vigil or Octave is sung, with the Commemorations that would have been made in the votive Mass, as in title VIII, n. 3 below. On other days on which such a Mass is impeded, as above or as in n. 9 below, but its Commemoration is not forbidden by that same title VIII, n. 3 (it is forbidden on the Commemoration of All the Faithful Departed and on a primary feast of our Lord Totum Duplex of the I class of the universal Church, except Easter and Whit Monday and Tuesday, when it is allowed), the Prayer of the impeded votive Mass is added under one conclusion in the sung Mass of the day, even the conventual Mass, and only the Commemorations proper to this Mass are made. Among the solemn votive Masses is counted the Mass of the Holy Ghost prescribed by the Constitutions at the election of the Most Reverend Master General and of a Prior Provincial or Conventual. On the Vigil of Pentecost, however, for the election of the Master General, the Mass of the day is sung with the Introit <b class=\"rb-inc\">Cum sanctificatus fuero</b>, one Prayer, the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria</b> and the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Credo</b>."],"tit-v-5":["Of Votive Masses","5. On the anniversaries of the election and coronation of the Supreme Pontiff the Prayer for the Pope is added in all Masses, according to the norms laid down in the next number."],"tit-v-6":["Of Votive Masses","6. On the anniversary of the election or translation and of the consecration of the Bishop, in all churches, those of Regulars too, provided the Commemoration of a votive Mass is not forbidden under n. 4, the Prayer for the Bishop is added in all conventual and private Masses, except Masses for the Dead, in the last place after the Prayers prescribed by the Rubrics."],"tit-v-7":["Of Votive Masses","7. If these anniversaries, of the Supreme Pontiff for the whole Church or of the Bishop for the whole diocese, are perpetually impeded, their Commemoration is made on the day to which they are transferred."],"tit-v-8":["Of Votive Masses","8. Sung votive Masses, even when not for a grave and public cause, are allowed on every day free of an Office Totum Duplex or Duplex, of any Sunday (even resumed, or transferred as to the Mass), and of a privileged Feria, Vigil or Octave."],"tit-v-9":["Of Votive Masses","9. All the Masses given above, and those to be listed below in title VII, nn. 1-5, even if the rite of the day would otherwise allow them, are forbidden in churches where the obligation of any conventual Mass presses which cannot be met by other priests; and in churches that have only one Mass they are also forbidden on 2 February, if the Candles are blessed, and on the Litanies, if the Procession is held."],"tit-vi-1":["Of Masses for the Dead","1. On the Commemoration of All the Faithful Departed three Masses for the Dead are allowed."],"tit-vi-2":["Of Masses for the Dead","2. On the first day of each week not impeded by a feast of three Lessons or above, by any Octave, by a Feria or Vigil with a Mass of its own, or by the resumption of an impeded Sunday Mass, the Mass for the Dead, that is, of Requiem, is always celebrated instead of the conventual Mass of the day. If the Requiem Mass cannot be celebrated within the week because of continuous feasts or the days just mentioned, then on another, less solemn day of the week the Prayer <b class=\"rb-inc\">Omnipotens</b> for the Living and the Dead is added, after the Prayers prescribed by the Rubrics, in the conventual Mass, which in convents of the First Order is nevertheless applied for the dead. This Mass is omitted, however, in the week in which the universal Church keeps the Commemoration of All the Faithful Departed, in Easter week and Whitsun week, in Holy Week and the week in which the Nativity of our Lord falls, when the funeral of a brother has already been held during the week, and lastly in the weeks in which the four Anniversaries of the Order noted in the Calendar are kept. These four Anniversaries may be sung on common feasts Totum Duplex and below, but not on feasts of obligation (even suppressed), Sundays, privileged Ferias, Vigils and Octaves, nor on feasts of the I and II class, even transferred; in that case they are transferred to the next day that is likewise not impeded. On the days on which the Mass of these four Anniversaries is sung, the conventual Mass of the day to be said in Choir is not omitted, except on the days on which, according to n. 2, a single conventual Mass of Requiem suffices. Where two conventual Masses cannot be celebrated, the single Mass of the feast is applied for the dead and the Prayer <b class=\"rb-inc\">Omnipotens</b> for the Living and the Dead is added after the Prayers prescribed by the Rubrics."],"tit-vi-3":["Of Masses for the Dead","3. In the church in which the funeral of someone deceased is held, even if the body is absent for a reasonable cause or already buried, one sung Mass for the day of death is allowed, or, for the poor, a read one. It is forbidden, however, on the primary feasts Totum Duplex of the I class of the universal Church (except Easter and Whit Monday and Tuesday, when it is allowed), on the feasts of the Dedication and Title of the very church in which the funeral is to be held, on that of the principal Patron of the place, and, for Religious Institutes, on the feasts of the Title or holy Founder of the Order or Congregation; provided these feasts have not been transferred that year as to their solemnity, in which case it is forbidden on the Sundays on which the external solemnity of such feasts is kept. Whenever this Mass is impeded by the Rubrics as above, it may be transferred to the nearest following day that is likewise not impeded. If the funeral is held on the Commemoration of the Faithful Departed, the first Mass is taken; and in churches and oratories where the first Mass has been celebrated solemnly, or is still to be celebrated separately for the Office of the day, the second or else the third Mass is said; but in that funeral Mass the Prayer for the same deceased, as for the day of death, is added under one conclusion."],"tit-vi-4":["Of Masses for the Dead","4. Likewise, in a church or public oratory where the funeral of the deceased is held solemnly, provided the Sacrifice is applied for that person, private Requiem Masses for the day of death may be said on that same day, as above, unless an Office occurs which impedes the Masses for the Dead indicated in the next number. Such private Masses may also be celebrated on one day of choice, between the death and the burial, in semi-public oratories which take the place of a church or public oratory where there is none; and daily during that time in semi-public oratories which do not take the place of a church or public oratory, and in the strictly private oratories of the house of the deceased, provided the body is physically present in the house where those oratories are, and an impeded day does not occur."],"tit-vi-5":["Of Masses for the Dead","5. On the third, seventh and thirtieth day and the anniversary from the death or burial of the deceased, and on a convenient day after news of the death has been received, one Mass for the deceased, as on the day of death, sung or even read, is allowed in any church; provided there does not occur a Sunday, a feast of obligation (even suppressed), the Commemoration of All the Faithful Departed, a feast Totum Duplex of the I or II class (even transferred), or a privileged Feria, Vigil or Octave. In that case such a Mass may be anticipated, if it can be, or transferred, to the nearest day likewise not impeded, provided it is sung."],"tit-vi-6":["Of Masses for the Dead","6. The same holds for a single sung Mass on Anniversaries celebrated by foundation on a day other than that of death, or held once each year for all the dead of some association. The same holds too for Masses sung for the devotion of the faithful during the eight days counted from the Commemoration of All the Faithful Departed inclusive; these Masses, however, cannot be anticipated or transferred outside those eight days."],"tit-vi-7":["Of Masses for the Dead","7. Likewise, in the church or principal public oratory of a cemetery, and indeed in any chapel of a cemetery rightly erected or to be erected, the Masses allowed to be celebrated there may be of Requiem; provided there does not occur a Sunday, a feast of obligation (even suppressed), a feast Totum Duplex of the I or II class (even transferred), or a privileged Feria, Vigil or Octave. This privilege does not extend to other churches or chapels outside the cemetery under which, even at the lawful distance, the body of someone deceased rests; nor to the churches, public oratories and chapels of a burial ground in which the dead were once buried but which has been abandoned for whatever cause, so that the dead are no longer usually buried there; nor to churches which, though they have a cemetery around them, have an obligation of Choir or the care of souls attached to them."],"tit-vi-8":["Of Masses for the Dead","8. Daily Masses for the Dead that are sung are allowed on every day on which the Office is not Totum Duplex or Duplex, and there does not occur a Sunday (even resumed, or transferred as to the Mass) or a privileged Feria, Vigil or Octave. Daily Masses for the Dead without chant are allowed only on days whose Office is of a Semiduplex or Simplex feast, of a day within a solemn or simple Octave, of the Friday after the Octave of the Ascension, of a greater Feria of Advent, of Holy Mary on Saturday, of a feast of three Lessons, or of a lesser Feria of the year; provided there does not occur a privileged Octave, an Ember day, a Rogation day, a Feria of the great Antiphons from 17 to 23 December inclusive, a Vigil, or a solemn, simple or three-Lesson Octave day, and that the Mass of an impeded Sunday is not first to be resumed. In Lent, daily Masses for the Dead without chant are allowed only on the first free day of each week, that is, one on which, outside the Ember days and Vigils, the Office is of a Semiduplex or Simplex feast or of a non-privileged Feria, according to the Calendar of the church in which the Mass is celebrated."],"tit-vi-9":["Of Masses for the Dead","9. Any Mass for the Dead, however, even on or for the day of death, is forbidden in churches where the Blessed Sacrament is exposed, for the whole time of the exposition, except the Masses of the Commemoration of All the Faithful Departed. It is also forbidden whenever the obligation of any conventual or parochial Mass presses which cannot be met by other priests; and in churches that have only one Mass it is forbidden on 2 February, Ash Wednesday, Palm Sunday and the Vigil of Pentecost, if the blessing of the Candles, the Ashes, the Palms or the baptismal Font respectively takes place, and on the days of the Litanies, if the Procession is held."],"tit-vii-1":["Of the Occurrence and Transfer of Feasts","1. If the feast of the principal Patron, or of the Title or Dedication of the church itself, or of the Title or holy Founder of the Order or Congregation, is accidentally impeded, one sung Mass of it is allowed on its own day, or a Commemoration in the sung Mass of the day, even the conventual Mass, according to what is said of solemn votive Masses in title V, n. 4."],"tit-vii-2":["Of the Occurrence and Transfer of Feasts","2. The same is observed in churches where, with a great concourse of people (of which the local Ordinary is the judge), a feast is kept which has to be transferred, commemorated or accidentally omitted, or a Mystery, Saint or Blessed who is mentioned that day in the Martyrology or in its appendix approved for the church concerned. Such a Mass, however, is said with the Commemorations proper to the rite Totum Duplex of the I or II class, if the feast is entered with that rite in the church&#x27;s own Calendar; otherwise with all the Commemorations befitting the rite of a common Totum Duplex and Duplex, according to the Rubrics."],"tit-vii-3":["Of the Occurrence and Transfer of Feasts","3. On the lesser Sundays of the year, in churches and in public or semi-public oratories where the external solemnity is kept of the feast of the principal Patron, of the Title or Dedication of the church itself, or of the Title or holy Founder of the Order or Congregation, which fell in the preceding week, the Mass of the transferred solemnity may be sung, and one read Mass of it is likewise allowed, unless a feast Totum Duplex of the I class occurs. If the external solemnity would have to be transferred to a greater Sunday, or to a Sunday impeded as above, Masses of the external solemnity are forbidden; but in the sung Mass of the day, even the conventual Mass, and in one other read Mass, a Commemoration of the external solemnity is made, according to the norms given for solemn votive Masses in title V, n. 4."],"tit-vii-4":["Of the Occurrence and Transfer of Feasts","4. Of common feasts Totum Duplex, Duplex, Semiduplex and Simplex which are perpetually or accidentally impeded, Masses may be said, even private ones, as the priest chooses; provided the impeding Office is not Totum Duplex of the I or II class, any Sunday (even resumed, or transferred as to the Mass), the Octave of the Epiphany, of Easter, of Pentecost or of Corpus Christi, the Octave day of Christmas or of the Ascension, or a privileged Feria or Vigil."],"tit-vii-5":["Of the Occurrence and Transfer of Feasts","5. On every day on which there does not occur an Office Totum Duplex or Duplex, any Sunday (even resumed, or transferred as to the Mass), a privileged Octave, a Feria of Lent from Ash Wednesday, of Passiontide, of the Ember days or the Rogations, or any Vigil, Masses may be said with the festal rite, even private ones, of any Office commemorated at Lauds, or of a Mystery, Saint or Blessed mentioned that day in the Martyrology or in its appendix approved for the church concerned; without prejudice to the wider privileges indicated in this title VII, nn. 1-4, and in title V, n. 8."],"tit-vii-6":["Of the Occurrence and Transfer of Feasts","6. All priests, secular and regular, are to celebrate their Masses, even those proper to Regulars, entirely according to the Calendar of the church or public oratory in which they celebrate; excluding, however, the particular rites proper to Orders and churches. The same is to be observed in a semi-public oratory, or the principal chapel of seminaries, colleges, pious communities, hospitals, prisons and the like. If, according to the Calendar of the church or of these oratories, votive or other Masses are allowed, they may celebrate them, observing the Rubrics and Decrees."],"tit-viii-1":["Of Commemorations","In making Commemorations the order laid down in the Office of the day and in the Rubrics of the Breviary is kept."],"tit-viii-2":["Of Commemorations","1. On all Sundays on which a Commemoration has been made of a feast Totum Duplex or Duplex, of a privileged Octave or of a solemn Octave day, the third Prayer is omitted in the Mass of the Sunday."],"tit-viii-3":["Of Commemorations","2. On the Vigil of Pentecost and on Palm Sunday nothing is done in the Mass of any Office commemorated at Lauds. Likewise on feasts of the I class nothing is done at the conventual or sung Mass of common feasts Totum Duplex and below, or of non-privileged Octaves commemorated at Lauds; nor on feasts of the II class of feasts of three Lessons or of Octaves of three Lessons or of a Commemoration only. All of these are commemorated only in private Masses. But feasts of our Lord falling on any lesser Sunday or on the Vigil of the Epiphany are commemorated even in the sung or conventual Masses of a feast Totum Duplex of the I class, if they were commemorated in the Office."],"tit-viii-4":["Of Commemorations","3. Where there is only one Mass and the obligation of a conventual Mass does not press: in solemn votive Masses for a grave and public cause, in Masses of an impeded feast Totum Duplex of the I class celebrated with a great concourse of people according to title VII, n. 2, and in Masses of the external solemnity of a feast Totum Duplex of the I class transferred to a Sunday according to title VII, n. 3, the only Commemorations made are of a feast Totum Duplex of the II class, of any Sunday (even anticipated), and, before the Commemoration of a lesser Sunday or of the Vigil of the Epiphany, of any feast of our Lord that occurs, of a greater Feria, of the Rogations, and of a privileged Vigil or Octave. In the Mass of an impeded feast Totum Duplex of the II class celebrated with a great concourse of people according to title VII, n. 2, and in Masses of the external solemnity of a feast Totum Duplex of the II class transferred to a Sunday, all the Commemorations occurring that day are made, except those of a day within a non-privileged Octave and of a feast or Octave day of three Lessons. Where there are several sung or conventual Masses, all the occurring Commemorations are said in the Mass of the day, or, if there is none, in the first of them; and in the solemn votive Masses and the others listed above only one Prayer is said."],"tit-ix-1":["Of the Office or Introit of the Mass, the Kyrie eleison and the Gloria in excelsis Deo","1. The Introit of the Mass and the Verse with the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria Patri</b> are always said in the same way, and after the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria</b> the Introit is always repeated up to the Verse; except in the Mass for the Dead, and from Passion Sunday to Maundy Thursday inclusive when the Mass is of the Season, when the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria Patri</b> is omitted and the Introit is repeated immediately after the Verse. The <b class=\"rb-inc\">Kyrie eleison</b> is said after the Introit alternately with the server: three times <b class=\"rb-inc\">Kyrie eleison</b>, three times <b class=\"rb-inc\">Christe eleison</b> and three times <b class=\"rb-inc\">Kyrie eleison</b>."],"tit-ix-2":["Of the Office or Introit of the Mass, the Kyrie eleison and the Gloria in excelsis Deo","2. The <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria in excelsis Deo</b> is said only when the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Te Deum laudamus</b> has been said in the Office before Lauds; except on Maundy Thursday and Holy Saturday, when the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria in excelsis</b> is said although the Te Deum was not said in the Office. Likewise in the conventual votive Masses customarily said through the week in our Order by privilege, according to title V, n. 1, but not in Septuagesima. In other Masses of a Vigil, or of any Feria that has its own Mass, even when they are celebrated on days on which the Te Deum was said in the Office, the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria in excelsis</b> is not said, for they are said according to their own rite; it is otherwise if the Mass of a Sunday is resumed on those days because it was impeded, or if private votive Masses are said, for these follow the rite of the day whose Office was said. Indeed in solemn votive Masses for a grave and public cause the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria in excelsis Deo</b> is said even on Ferias."],"tit-x-1":["Of the number and order of the Prayers ordinarily said, especially at the conventual Mass","At the conventual Mass only three Prayers are said, unless the number of Commemorations requires more; in that case as many Prayers are said as Commemorations were made at Lauds, and in the same order."],"tit-x-2":["Of the number and order of the Prayers ordinarily said, especially at the conventual Mass","1. In conventual and private Masses of the rite Semiduplex and below (ferial ones too), and in votive Masses not for a grave and public cause, unless a Commemoration has been made of a feast Totum Duplex, Duplex or of a solemn Octave, the Prayers that complete the number three are taken from the first Sunday of the season concerned, as follows: a) In Advent, when a Commemoration has been made, the third Prayer is of Our Lady, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Deus qui de beatae Mariae</b>; unless a Commemoration of Our Lady is to be said, for then the third Prayer is <b class=\"rb-inc\">Deus qui caritatis</b>. b) From 2 January to the Octave day of the Epiphany exclusive, every day (except on the feast of the Holy Family), after the other Commemorations, the Prayer of Our Lady <b class=\"rb-inc\">Deus qui salutis</b> is said. On 4 January the second Prayer is of Our Lady, the third for the Pope. On the Vigil of the Epiphany the Prayers are said as in the Missal. c) In Paschaltide up to the Vigil of the Ascension inclusive, when a Commemoration has been made, the third Prayer is of the Cross, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Deus qui pro nobis Filium</b>, which, having been said at Lauds, is also added when several Commemorations are to be said. d) From the Octave of the Epiphany to Maundy Thursday, and from the Octave of the Sacred Heart to Advent, when a Commemoration has been made, the third Prayer is <b class=\"rb-inc\">A cunctis</b>; unless a Commemoration of Our Lady or of All Saints is to be said, for in that case the third Prayer is at choice; and in Septuagesima, up to Maundy Thursday, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Deus qui caritatis</b>. e) On these days, when two or more Commemorations have been made, no Prayer is added from the Sunday, except in the cases noted under b) and c). If no Commemoration is to be said, the second and third Prayers are taken from the first Sunday of the season concerned; but then in the votive Masses of Our Lady (on Saturday), instead of the second or third Prayer, of Our Lady or <b class=\"rb-inc\">A cunctis</b>, the Prayer for the Church, n. 7, is taken. f) On the privileged Vigils of the Nativity of our Lord and of the Epiphany the Prayers are said as in the Missal. On Palm Sunday and on the Vigil of Pentecost only one Prayer is said, even if some Commemoration was made at Lauds. g) In votive Masses not for a grave and public cause the Commemoration of the Office of the day is always added in the second place, unless a Commemoration inseparable from the Mass must come before it. h) When two conventual Masses occur, these Prayers are also used to complete the number three, although they may then have to be said in both Masses."],"tit-x-3":["Of the number and order of the Prayers ordinarily said, especially at the conventual Mass","2. On the anniversary of his own ordination to the priesthood, counted from the fixed day of the month, if the Vigil of Christmas or of Pentecost, Palm Sunday or a feast Totum Duplex of the I class does not occur (otherwise on the nearest following day free of a feast Totum Duplex of the I class), any priest may add, except in Masses for the Dead and after the Prayers prescribed by the Rubrics, the Prayer for the priest himself, as among the various Prayers."],"tit-x-4":["Of the number and order of the Prayers ordinarily said, especially at the conventual Mass","3. Prayers or Collects ordered by the Ordinary are never to be more than two; they are put, never under one conclusion with the Prayer of the Mass, in the last place after the Prayers prescribed by the Rubrics, or in place of the Prayer at choice assigned for the various seasons, namely whenever the third Prayer is at choice. They must be omitted, however, because of the Mass or Commemoration concerned, on all feasts Totum Duplex of the I and II class, on greater Sundays, on privileged Ferias, Vigils and Octaves, in votive Masses celebrated for a grave and public cause or after the manner of solemn ones, as above, and whenever four Prayers have already been said in the Mass according to the Rubrics; moreover Collects for the Dead must also be omitted in Paschaltide and in all Masses celebrated of an Office Simplex and above or with its Commemoration. And if the Prayer against the persecutors of the Church or for the Pope has been ordered, on days on which either is prescribed by the Rubrics, both commands are fulfilled with one Prayer. If, however, by the Ordinary&#x27;s command such Collects are to be said for a grave cause, they are omitted only on the Vigils of Christmas and Pentecost, on Palm Sunday and on all feasts Totum Duplex of the I class. If they are expressly ordered even on feasts Totum Duplex of the I class, they are then omitted on Christmas, the Epiphany, Maundy Thursday, Holy Saturday, Easter Sunday, the Ascension, Pentecost, the feasts of the Holy Trinity, Corpus Christi, the Sacred Heart and Christ the King."],"tit-x-5":["Of the number and order of the Prayers ordinarily said, especially at the conventual Mass","4. The order in saying these Collects (apart from the Collect for the Dead, which is always put in the last place but one among the Prayers, those allowed at the celebrant&#x27;s choice included) is this: first the Collect to be said for a grave cause, if there is one. If both Collects are ordered for a grave cause, first, in the order of the Litanies, the Collect of a Mystery or Saint that was prescribed; then the one to be taken from the various votive Masses or from the Prayers at the end of the Missal, keeping, if both are of this kind, the order in which they stand in the Missal."],"tit-x-6":["Of the number and order of the Prayers ordinarily said, especially at the conventual Mass","5. In all read Masses (except the conventual Mass) celebrated on a day of the rite of three Lessons, outside the greater privileged Ferias, whether of the occurring Office or chosen from the votive Masses for a cause that is not grave and public, more Prayers may be said at the celebrant&#x27;s choice; always taking care that such Prayers 1) come after the Prayers prescribed by the Rubrics and the Collects ordered by the Ordinary, and together with them do not exceed seven, and keep or make an odd number; 2) keep among themselves the order laid down in n. 4 above for two Collects, when both are for a grave cause or both not."],"tit-xi-1":["How the Prayers are concluded","1. Whenever several Prayers are said, only the first and the last are finished with their peroration or conclusion; <b class=\"rb-inc\">Oremus</b> is said only before the first and the second Prayer; and before the first <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b> is also said, unless otherwise noted, and except at the Secrets, where instead of it <b class=\"rb-inc\">Domine exaudi orationem meam</b> is said before the first Secret."],"tit-xi-2":["How the Prayers are concluded","2. In the peroration or conclusion of the Prayers this way of ending is kept: if the Prayer is addressed to the Father, it ends <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per Dominum nostrum</b>; if to the Son, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Qui vivis et regnas cum Deo Patre</b>; if the Son is mentioned at the beginning of the Prayer, it ends <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per eumdem Dominum nostrum</b>; if He is mentioned at the end, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Qui tecum vivit</b>; if the Holy Ghost has been mentioned in the Prayer, it ends <b class=\"rb-inc\">in unitate ejusdem Spiritus Sancti</b>; lastly, if Blessed Mary is mentioned as Mother of God, it ends <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per eumdem Dominum</b>, or <b class=\"rb-inc\">Qui tecum vivit</b>."],"tit-xii-1":["Of the Prayer in Masses for the Dead","1. In all the Masses listed in title VI, nn. 3-7, and whenever Mass for the Dead is celebrated solemnly, that is, with the rite corresponding to a Duplex, only one Prayer is said; in the others three are said, in the order given below in n. 4 and following."],"tit-xii-2":["Of the Prayer in Masses for the Dead","2. On the day of death or burial of the deceased, and on a convenient day after news of the death has been received: for a Pope the Prayer is <b class=\"rb-inc\">Deus, qui inter summos</b>, which is also said on his Anniversary; for a Bishop or a priest, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Deus, qui inter apostolicos</b>, saying <b class=\"rb-inc\">pontificali</b> for a Bishop and <b class=\"rb-inc\">sacerdotali</b> for a priest; for any other man or woman, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Deus, cui proprium</b>."],"tit-xii-3":["Of the Prayer in Masses for the Dead","3. On the third, seventh and thirtieth day from the burial, for a Pope, a Bishop or a priest the Prayer is as noted for the day of death in n. 2; for any other man or woman it is <b class=\"rb-inc\">Quaesumus Domine, ut animae</b>."],"tit-xii-4":["Of the Prayer in Masses for the Dead","4. If Mass is celebrated specially for one deceased person on a day other than that of death or burial: for a Pope, a Bishop or a priest the first Prayer is as on the day of their death, the second <b class=\"rb-inc\">Deus, veniae largitor</b>, the third <b class=\"rb-inc\">Omnipotens sempiterne Deus, qui vivorum</b>. For anyone else the first is, for a man, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Inclina, Domine</b>; for a woman, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Quaesumus, Domine, pro tua</b>; the second and third are unchanged."],"tit-xii-5":["Of the Prayer in Masses for the Dead","5. If Mass is celebrated for all the dead in general, the first Prayer is <b class=\"rb-inc\">Fidelium Deus</b>, the second and third as in n. 4. If for several only, the first Prayer is <b class=\"rb-inc\">Animabus, quaesumus</b>, or <b class=\"rb-inc\">Propitiare, quaesumus</b>; the second and third are unchanged. If it is not known for whom Mass is to be celebrated, the first Prayer is <b class=\"rb-inc\">Deus, qui inter Apostolicos</b>; the second and third are unchanged."],"tit-xii-6":["Of the Prayer in Masses for the Dead","6. If Mass is celebrated for the brethren, familiars and benefactors on the first unimpeded day of the week, according to title VI, n. 2, the first Prayer is <b class=\"rb-inc\">Deus, veniae largitor</b>, the second <b class=\"rb-inc\">Deus, qui nos Patrem</b>; the third is unchanged."],"tit-xii-7":["Of the Prayer in Masses for the Dead","7. In the four Anniversaries customarily kept every year in our Order, the first Prayer is <b class=\"rb-inc\">Deus Indulgentiarum</b>, in the plural; the second and third as in n. 4, except in two of them: in the February one the second Prayer is <b class=\"rb-inc\">Deus, qui nos Patrem</b>, and in the July one the second is <b class=\"rb-inc\">Deus, cujus miseratione</b>; the third is unchanged."],"tit-xii-8":["Of the Prayer in Masses for the Dead","8. If the day of death or burial of someone deceased and an Anniversary fall together, the first Prayer is said for the deceased now present, the second for the Anniversary."],"tit-xii-9":["Of the Prayer in Masses for the Dead","9. If a funeral Mass with the body present is to be celebrated on All Souls&#x27; Day, it is the first Mass of the Commemoration of All the Faithful Departed, with the Prayer for the deceased, as on the day of death, added under one conclusion."],"tit-xiii-1":["Of the Epistle, the Gradual or Responsory, the Alleluia, the Tract and the Sequence, and of the Gospel","1. After the last Prayer the Epistle is said, and when it is finished the ministers make no answer. After the Epistle the Gradual, which with us is called the Responsory, is always said, except from the Saturday before Low Sunday inclusive to the Octave of Pentecost; in its place, through all that time, in Masses of the Season and of any feast whatever, two Verses are said with four Alleluias, in this order: before the first Verse two Alleluias, and after the Verse another Alleluia; then the second Verse, and after it another Alleluia. When the Sequence is said, the Alleluia is not said after the last Verse but after the Sequence. Excepted are feasts of three Lessons, the Vigil of the Ascension and ferial days, on which Alleluia is said only once before the single Verse and once after it; on Ferias this Verse is to be taken alternately from the two given for the Sunday."],"tit-xiii-2":["Of the Epistle, the Gradual or Responsory, the Alleluia, the Tract and the Sequence, and of the Gospel","2. Also excepted are the Masses of the Rogations, in which only one Alleluia is said before the Verse, and it is not repeated after it."],"tit-xiii-3":["Of the Epistle, the Gradual or Responsory, the Alleluia, the Tract and the Sequence, and of the Gospel","3. Similarly, after each Lesson on the Wednesday and Saturday of the Ember days of Pentecost the Alleluias are said in the way marked there."],"tit-xiii-4":["Of the Epistle, the Gradual or Responsory, the Alleluia, the Tract and the Sequence, and of the Gospel","4. From the feast of the Holy Trinity inclusive to Septuagesima exclusive, when the Responsory is said, three Alleluias are said: twice before the last Verse and once after the Verse (if the Sequence is said, it is done as above); and this on Sundays as well as on all feasts and Octaves, and in the votive Masses customarily said through the week in our Order by privilege or custom."],"tit-xiii-5":["Of the Epistle, the Gradual or Responsory, the Alleluia, the Tract and the Sequence, and of the Gospel","5. On feasts of three Lessons only two Alleluias are said, with a single Verse, after the Responsory: once before the Verse and once after it."],"tit-xiii-6":["Of the Epistle, the Gradual or Responsory, the Alleluia, the Tract and the Sequence, and of the Gospel","6. On Ferias they are omitted altogether, as also in the Mass of the Sunday said on those ferial days, and on the feast of the Innocents when it does not fall on a Sunday; likewise on Ember days and Vigils, on whatever day they are kept, except the Vigil of Christmas when it falls on a Sunday and the Vigil of the Epiphany on whatever day it falls, on which the Alleluias are said as they are set in those Masses."],"tit-xiii-7":["Of the Epistle, the Gradual or Responsory, the Alleluia, the Tract and the Sequence, and of the Gospel","7. From Septuagesima to Holy Saturday exclusive, in Masses of the Season and of feasts alike, no Alleluia is said; but after the Responsory and its single Verse, the Alleluia and the following Verse being left out, the Tract is said; except on feasts of three Lessons, on which it is never said, and in the Sunday Masses said on ferial days from Septuagesima to Ash Wednesday. Also excepted are certain Ferias from Ash Wednesday and in Lent which have no Tract. Several Tracts are said on Holy Saturday and on the Vigil of Pentecost after the Lessons, as assigned there; likewise on the Ember Saturdays of Advent and September."],"tit-xiii-8":["Of the Epistle, the Gradual or Responsory, the Alleluia, the Tract and the Sequence, and of the Gospel","8. Whenever the Tract is said, the Sequence is never said (except on the Compassion of the Blessed Virgin Mary); the Sequence is said on the days on which it is set down to be said."],"tit-xiii-9":["Of the Epistle, the Gradual or Responsory, the Alleluia, the Tract and the Sequence, and of the Gospel","9. The Sequence for the Dead is said in all Masses with a single Prayer, and in the daily Masses that are sung and in conventual ones; in other daily read Masses it may be said or omitted at the celebrant&#x27;s choice."],"tit-xiii-10":["Of the Epistle, the Gradual or Responsory, the Alleluia, the Tract and the Sequence, and of the Gospel","10. After the Responsory, the Alleluia, the Tract or the Sequence, the Gospel is said, beginning with <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b>. ℟. <b class=\"rb-inc\">Et cum spiritu tuo</b>. Then: <b class=\"rb-inc\">Sequentia sancti Evangelii secundum</b>, etc. ℟. <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria tibi Domine</b>. After the Gospel the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Credo</b> is said, if it is to be said, as noted in the next title."],"tit-xiv-1":["When the Credo is to be said at Mass","The <b class=\"rb-inc\">Credo</b>, which is called the Symbol of the Apostles, is said: on Christmas Day at all the Masses, and daily through the Octave, even on the feasts of Saints; on the Circumcision; on the Epiphany and through its Octave; on Maundy Thursday; on Easter Day and through its Octave; on the Ascension and through its Octave; on Pentecost and through its Octave; on Corpus Christi and through its Octave; on the feast of the Sacred Heart and through its Octave; on all Sundays of the year, even when the Office is of a feast on which it would not otherwise be said; on all feasts of Our Lady; on the feasts of the twelve Apostles and of the Evangelists, and on the Octave day of the Apostles Peter and Paul; on both feasts of the Chair of St Peter and on St Peter&#x27;s Chains; on the feasts of the Conversion and of the Commemoration of St Paul the Apostle; on the feast of St John before the Latin Gate; on the feast of St Barnabas the Apostle; on the feasts of the Finding and of the Exaltation of the Holy Cross; on the feasts of the Holy Name of Jesus and of the Holy Family; on the feast of the Crown of our Lord; on the feast of the Most Precious Blood; on the Transfiguration; on the feasts of the Angels; on both feasts of St Joseph, Spouse of the Blessed Virgin Mary; on the feast of St Mary Magdalen; on the three feasts of our blessed Father Dominic; on each feast of the canonized Saints of our Order; on the three feasts of St Catherine of Siena; on the principal feast of St Francis of Assisi; on the feasts of the Doctors of the Church, such as Augustine, Jerome, Gregory the Pope, Leo the Great, on the feast of St Ambrose, on both feasts of St Thomas Aquinas and on the feast of St Bonaventure, with the feasts added of St Hilary, St Francis de Sales, St Peter Damian, St Isidore, St Anselm, St Bede the Venerable, St Alphonsus Mary de Liguori and St Bernard; likewise on the feasts of the Doctors John Chrysostom, Athanasius, Cyril of Alexandria, Cyril of Jerusalem, John Damascene and Ephrem the Syrian, Gregory Nazianzen and Basil; on the feast of All Saints; on the Dedication of the Holy Saviour and of the Apostles Peter and Paul; on the feast of the Dedication of the church itself and on its Anniversary; on the day of the Consecration of a church or altar; on the day of the Election and of the Coronation of the Supreme Pontiff; on each feast of the Patrons of the place or of the Title of our churches; and in the church itself where the body or a notable relic is kept of the Saint whose Mass is celebrated. Likewise if any Mass is to be celebrated on a day on which the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Credo</b> must be sung, unless it is for the Dead or of the Vigil of a Saint. On other days it is not said, except in solemn votive Masses, or of any Feria with a Mass of its own, celebrated for a grave and public cause or necessity."],"tit-xv-1":["Of the Offertory, the Secrets, the Prefaces and the Canon","1. After the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Credo</b>, or, if it is not said, after the Gospel, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b>. <b class=\"rb-inc\">Oremus</b> is said. Then the Offertory is said; after it the oblation is made with its Prayer, as noted in the manner of saying Mass. When the oblation is made, and the Prayer after the washing of the fingers is finished with <b class=\"rb-inc\">Orate fratres</b>, the secret Prayers are said, as many as the Prayers said aloud at the beginning of Mass; before the first Prayer <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b> is not said, but <b class=\"rb-inc\">Domine exaudi orationem meam: et clamor meus ad te veniat</b>, and <b class=\"rb-inc\">Oremus</b>; before the second only <b class=\"rb-inc\">Oremus</b>. The first and the last Prayer are concluded as said above in the Rubric on the Prayers, and they are said secretly up to the words <b class=\"rb-inc\">Per omnia saecula saeculorum</b>, which are said aloud; and in the same voice the Preface is begun at once and continued."],"tit-xv-2":["Of the Offertory, the Secrets, the Prefaces and the Canon","2. The Prefaces are said as prescribed in their proper places for the order to be kept in the Masses; and those assigned as proper to Lent, Passiontide, Paschaltide and the Octaves are also said on feasts celebrated in those times, unless those feasts have a Preface of their own. The Prefaces of solemn Octaves, however, are never said in the Mass of a Sunday, except when it is resumed during the week. If within the Octave of a feast that has its own Preface another, greater feast occurs which has none, the Preface of the Octave is said. Likewise in votive Masses the proper Preface is said if they have one; if not, the Preface of the Season or of the Octave within which such Masses happen to be celebrated; otherwise the common Preface. On all Sundays outside Lent, Paschaltide and the most solemn Octaves, the Preface of the Trinity is said. In the Mass of a Sunday resumed during the week, the Preface of the Octave or the common Preface is said. When a votive Mass is celebrated for a public cause, the Preface is sung in the solemn chant, as on a Totum Duplex. In Masses for the Dead, at any time, their own Preface is always said."],"tit-xv-3":["Of the Offertory, the Secrets, the Prefaces and the Canon","3. After the Preface the Canon of the Mass begins, to be said secretly, in which everything is done as in the Order of Mass. Where, after the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Memento</b>, the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Communicantes</b> and <b class=\"rb-inc\">Hanc igitur</b> or other parts vary, this is noted in its place in the proper Masses."],"tit-xvi-1":["Of the Communion, the Prayers after Communion, Ite missa est or Benedicamus Domino, the Blessing and the Gospel of St John","1. When the Canon and everything else up to the Communion are finished, and the Communion is made, the Communion and the Prayers after Communion are said in the same manner and order as at the beginning of Mass; and after the last, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b>. ℟. <b class=\"rb-inc\">Et cum spiritu tuo</b> is said again; and then <b class=\"rb-inc\">Ite missa est</b> or <b class=\"rb-inc\">Benedicamus Domino</b>, as the time requires. <b class=\"rb-inc\">Ite missa est</b> is said whenever the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria in excelsis</b> has been said; when it has not, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Benedicamus Domino</b> is said in its place. ℟. <b class=\"rb-inc\">Deo gratias</b>. In Masses for the Dead, <b class=\"rb-inc\">Requiescant in pace</b>. ℟. <b class=\"rb-inc\">Amen</b>. Then, after the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Placeat</b>, the blessing is given, which the celebrant always gives at Mass, except in Masses for the Dead."],"tit-xvi-2":["Of the Communion, the Prayers after Communion, Ite missa est or Benedicamus Domino, the Blessing and the Gospel of St John","2. Then, after <b class=\"rb-inc\">Dominus vobiscum</b> and ℟. <b class=\"rb-inc\">Et cum spiritu tuo</b>, the Gospel of St John <b class=\"rb-inc\">In principio</b> is read, preceded by <b class=\"rb-inc\">Initium sancti Evangelii</b>, etc. Note that the Gospel <b class=\"rb-inc\">In principio erat Verbum</b> is always said at the end of every Mass, except at the third Mass of Christmas, in place of which the Gospel <b class=\"rb-inc\">Cum natus esset Jesus</b> is said; and except on ferial days all through Lent when the Mass is not of the Feria (but not at the conventual Mass), for then the Gospel of the current Feria is said; and on Palm Sunday, in private Masses, the Gospel placed after the blessing of the palms is read; and when on Sundays the Mass is of a feast and not of the Sunday, for then the Gospel of the Sunday is to be said at the end, except on the fourth Sunday of Advent (if the Vigil of Christmas falls on it), whose Gospel is then not read at the end. The last Gospel is likewise of the Sunday in all Masses in which an anticipated Sunday has been commemorated; similarly, on the Ember days of September and Advent and on the Rogation days, when the Mass is of a feast or a votive Mass is read, the Gospel of the Feria is said at the end, except when on the Ember Wednesday of Advent a Mass of Our Lady is said which has the same Gospel as that Wednesday. On the Vigils of Saints that fall on feasts or Ember days, the Gospel of the Vigil is not read at the end of Mass. In Masses for the Dead the Gospel <b class=\"rb-inc\">In principio</b> is always read."],"tit-xvii-1":["Of ordering the Mass according to these Rubrics","Knowing these Rubrics, the priest who is to celebrate and wishes to order the Mass of the day should turn to the Masses of the Season or of the Saints, according to the time; if the Mass has no proper, to the Common of Saints. If the Feria is to be kept and has no Mass of its own, the Mass of the preceding Sunday is said, without the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Gloria in excelsis</b> and the <b class=\"rb-inc\">Credo</b>, and with one Verse with Alleluia after the Responsory; except in Paschaltide, when on those days a single Verse is said with two Alleluias, as noted above. If a votive Mass is to be said, it is found in its place after the Common of Saints. If several Prayers are to be said, they are found in their places, each for its subject, before and after the Proper of Saints and after the votive Masses. If Mass is to be celebrated for the Dead, it is found after the votive Masses, with the various Prayers, towards the end of the Missal."],"tit-xviii-1":["Of the hour of celebrating Mass","A private Mass may be said at any hour of the day from dawn until noon. The conventual and high Mass, from Easter to the feast of the Exaltation of the Holy Cross exclusive, is said after Terce has been said in Choir; except on Fridays, on the Ember days within the Octave of Pentecost and on fasting Vigils, even when they are solemn days, on which the Mass is to be sung after Sext. From the Exaltation of the Holy Cross to Ash Wednesday exclusive it is always to be sung after Sext; and from Ash Wednesday to Easter Sunday it is sung after None; except on Sundays, on which, all the year round, Mass is sung after Terce."],"tit-xix-1":["Of the uniformity to be kept in celebrating Mass","All the ceremonies of our Order, at the altar, in choir and in the divine Office, are to be observed by all in conformity and uniformity. So in Mass, private or common, nothing is to be put in that is not marked in our Ordinarium, neither at the beginning nor at the end; and let all beware of adding to, taking from, transposing or changing anything in the Mass or our Office out of private spirit, whether in words or in rite, secretly or aloud."],"tit-xx-1":["Of the altar and the other things required for the Sacrifice","1. The altar on which the most holy Sacrifice of the Mass is celebrated must be of stone and consecrated by a Bishop, or by an Abbot having the faculty from the Apostolic See; or at least there must be a polished altar-stone (called a portable altar), likewise consecrated by a Bishop or Abbot as above, whole, or not broken in its greater part, and large enough to hold the host and the greater part of the chalice. It is to be fitly let into the table of the altar, placed in the middle between the two horns, not more than about a palm&#x27;s breadth from the front; and so set into the altar that it stands a little above the surface of the table, so that the celebrant can tell it by touch."],"tit-xx-2":["Of the altar and the other things required for the Sacrifice","2. The table of the altar, above all a consecrated one, is to be decently adorned and clean, and covered with three clean linen cloths blessed by a Bishop or another who has the power, the uppermost at least long enough to reach to the ground, the other two shorter, or one folded double; and they are to have a cover of leather or of coarser coloured cloth to keep the cloths from dust and other dirt."],"tit-xx-3":["Of the altar and the other things required for the Sacrifice","3. On the altar the cross of Christ crucified is set in the middle, and at least two candlesticks with lighted candles, one on either side of it; and at the foot of the cross is placed a card containing the words of consecration."],"tit-xx-4":["Of the altar and the other things required for the Sacrifice","4. At the Epistle horn a pillow or cushion is placed to set the Missal on; and on the same side a candle is put, to be lit at the consecration and elevation of the Sacrament, and kept lit by the server until the communion is done."],"tit-xx-5":["Of the altar and the other things required for the Sacrifice","5. At the Epistle horn there is also to be a small bell, with which the people are warned by a few strokes before the elevation."],"tit-xx-6":["Of the altar and the other things required for the Sacrifice","6. The cruets of wine and water are not to be of brass or another base metal, but of silver or pewter, or better of crystal or clear glass; clean of all dirt, each fitly closed with its lid of pewter or silver; and with a small basin and a clean towel they are set on the same side in a niche or on a small table prepared for it. Nothing at all is to be put on the altar or kept under it that does not belong to the Sacrifice of the Mass or to the adornment of the altar itself."],"tit-xx-7":["Of the altar and the other things required for the Sacrifice","7. The altar cloths, the small towels and the other things set apart for the use of the altars and of the priests are to be washed often; and the purificators are to be white and very clean."],"tit-xx-8":["Of the altar and the other things required for the Sacrifice","8. Corporals are to be of linen, pure and white, plain and unworked, torn in no part, and with no silk woven in; they may only be hemmed with simple needlework. They are not to be noticeably small or large, but of a fitting size, so that they do not exceed four folds in length and three in width."],"tit-xx-9":["Of the altar and the other things required for the Sacrifice","9. They are kept in burses (as they are called), lined inside with silk or fine white cloth; the burse is laid on the chalice veil when the chalice is carried to the altar, and has a cross or another holy image worked on one side."]};
